/**
 * brands, long-form SEO overrides
 */
module.exports = {
  "myfather": "<p><strong>My Father Cigars</strong> is the García family's company and factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">José \"Pepín\" García</a>, born in Cuba in 1950, rolled his first cigar at 11. In 2003 he opened El Rey de los Habanos, a small factory in Miami's Little Havana, with his son <a href=\"/node/jaimegarcia/\">Jaime</a> and daughter Janny. Its first client was Pete Johnson's <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a>, and the Garcías released their own Don Pepín García line the same year.</p>\n<p>Demand pushed the family into a modest Nicaraguan factory, and then into a much bigger one. In 2008 Jaime blended the My Father cigar as a tribute to his father. The new Estelí factory took that name; it rolled its first test cigars in June 2009 and formally opened on August 29, 2009. A small operation in Doral, Florida, still rolls some cigars.</p>\n<p>The house brands include My Father, The Judge, Le Bijou 1922 and Flor de las Antillas, its best seller. Cigar Aficionado named The Judge Grand Robusto its 2024 Cigar of the Year. The factory also makes cigars for other companies: Tatuaje, <a href=\"/node/latelier/\">L'Atelier</a>, <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>, <a href=\"/node/foundation/\">Foundation</a>'s The Wise Man, and Ashton's <a href=\"/node/sancristobal/\">San Cristóbal</a> and <a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a>. In 2022 the Garcías made more than 20 million cigars.</p>",
  "tatuaje": "<p><strong>Tatuaje</strong> (Spanish for tattoo) is the boutique brand <a href=\"/node/petejohnson/\">Pete Johnson</a> launched in <strong>2003</strong>. Johnson came out of Los Angeles cigar retail and named the marca for his tattoos. That year he met <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a>, who rolled the first Tatuajes at <a href=\"/node/elreyhabanos/\">El Rey de los Habanos</a> in Miami's Little Havana. Tatuaje was Pepín's first private-label client, and the cigars helped introduce García Cuban-style construction to a new wave of U.S. smokers. Who makes Tatuaje? The García family does. Pete owns the brand. Pepín, Jaime, and the factory roll it.</p>\n<p>When the Garcías built <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí, Tatuaje moved with them. Core lines include Brown Label (Selección de Cazador), Black Label, Havana VI, Fausto, Cojonú, and Reserva, plus years of limiteds that taught boutique smokers to hunt boxes. The style is Cuban-inspired: triple cap, slide-lid cabinets, full Nicaraguan flavor. Johnson's later sister marca, <a href=\"/node/latelier/\">L'Atelier</a> (2012), is a separate catalog (Sancti Spíritus leaf, wine-world names), also made with the Garcías. Tatuaje is the tattoo-shop brand. L'Atelier is the workshop brand. Neither is an STG or Altadis name.</p>\n<p>The García family has rolled every Tatuaje, including the Honduran Series P made at My Father's Honduras factory. The through-line does not change. If you are asking where Tatuaje is made, start at El Rey for the origin story and My Father in Estelí for the cigars on shelves today. Crowned Heads and other García clients share that factory culture. Tatuaje is still Pete's marca on García manufacturing.</p>",
  "padron": "<p><strong>Padrón</strong> is one of the most respected names in premium cigars: a family-owned Nicaraguan house founded in <strong>1964</strong> in Miami by Cuban émigré <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a>. After the Revolution ended the family's Pinar del Río tobacco life, Orlando started with a handful of rollers and a hammer he kept as a reminder of hard work, then bet early on Nicaraguan leaf. By <strong>1970</strong> production had moved to Estelí at what is now <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a>. The company survived civil war, factory disasters, and embargo-era chaos without abandoning its model: grow and age its own tobacco, refuse flash marketing, sell through tight retailer relationships, and let consistency do the talking.</p>\n<p>The brand ladder is simple and famous. The numbered regular line (2000 through 7000 and related sizes) is the everyday Padrón puro. <a href=\"/node/padron1964/\">1964 Anniversary</a> marks the founding year with multi-year aged leaf and box-pressed elegance. <a href=\"/node/padron1926/\">Serie 1926</a> honors Orlando's birth year at the top of the pyramid. Natural and Maduro wrappers run across the range; almost everything is a Nicaraguan puro built from family inventory that often ages for years before it is rolled, then ages again as finished cigars. Leadership today includes <a href=\"/node/jorgepadron/\">Jorge Padrón</a> (president) and family partners such as <a href=\"/node/orlandopadron/\">Orlando Padrón</a>, continuing the no-sales-force culture José Orlando set until his death in 2017.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Padrón is the benchmark Nicaraguan family puro node.</p>",
  "rojas": "<p><strong>Rojas Cigars</strong> is the boutique brand founded by <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a>. Lines include <a href=\"/node/guayacan/\">Guayacan</a>, Unfinished Business, Street Tacos, KSG, and Quiet Morning. KSG stands for \"King of Small Gauge,\" Rojas's nickname for himself, and it reflects his focus on thinner vitolas. The first KSG, a 7 1/2 x 38 lancero, debuted in April 2020 with tobacco Rojas bought in 2019 from Somoto, a lesser-known region near the Honduran border. A 6 1/2 x 42 KSG Limited Edition 2026 is due in November 2026.</p>\n<p>The Street Tacos series, inspired by Mexican street food, debuted in November 2020. It has since added an annual Cinco de Mayo limited edition (2024, 2025, and 2026), Al Pastor (2025), and Mictlán, a 2026 Día de los Muertos release. One early collaboration, the Street Tacos Elote Firecracker made for United Cigars, was rolled at Tabacalera Flor de San Luis in Nicaragua. Quiet Morning, a regular-production line pitched as the first cigar of the day, arrived at PCA 2026.</p>\n<p>Current Rojas cigars are made at the <a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas Cigar Factory</a>, a converted movie theater in Estelí. Rojas says all the filler and binder comes from his own farms in Condega and Jalapa. The same factory makes <a href=\"/node/stolenthrone/\">Stolen Throne</a>, and earlier Rojas lines came from <a href=\"/node/neworder/\">New Order of the Ages</a> (2016–18).</p>",
  "guayacan": "<p><strong>Guayacan</strong> is the brand that introduced <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a> to the U.S. market. When he first showed it at the 2013 IPCPR show, Rojas owned and ran his own Estelí factory, Tabacalera Aromas de Jalapa. Lines include Guayacan Habano and Maduro, Sabor de Estelí and Gallo Pinto.</p>\n<p>After the partners at Aromas de Jalapa split in late 2014, Rojas and Renegade Cigars owner Brandon Hayes opened New Order of the Ages (NOA), a 5,000-square-foot Estelí factory, on January 1, 2016. Guayacan moved there. NOA also made boutique brands such as Ezra Zion, Que Vida and <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a>, which later moved to <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragón</a>. On the map, NOA appears as <a href=\"/node/neworder/\">New Order</a>.</p>\n<p>Guayacan Rojas Reserve arrived in 2015, followed by a Connecticut version and the Rojas Cabinet Selection. The line now sits in the <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas Cigars</a> portfolio. Rojas later converted an old Estelí movie theater into the <a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas Cigar Factory</a>. That path, from a small factory of his own to a partnership with a brand owner and then a converted theater, is a common one among owner-run Estelí shops such as <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a>.</p>\n<p>Earlier versions of this map tied Guayacan to A.J. Fernández, San Lotano and My Father. We found no source for any of them, so those links have been removed.</p>",
  "warped": "<p><strong>Warped Cigars</strong> is the boutique brand of <a href=\"/node/kylegellis/\">Kyle Gellis</a>, based in Jupiter, Florida. Industry reports say Gellis started it in 2009 at 19, while studying marketing at the University of Central Florida. The company's own site gives 2007. He learned the trade making weekly trips to Miami's Little Havana, and he settled on <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a> on Calle Ocho, where he learned from Willy Herrera, the rollers and the factory manager. His first IPCPR lineup was Flor del Valle, El Oso and La Colmena.</p>\n<p>Miami-made cigars were expensive, so El Titan introduced Gellis to <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a>, the Estelí factory of <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa</a>, then called Casa Fernández. <strong>Flor del Valle</strong>, announced in July 2014, was Warped's first cigar not made in Miami. It has a Jalapa Corojo '99 wrapper and Corojo '99 and Criollo '98 binders. <strong>Futuro</strong> came from a blending project with Aganorsa's <a href=\"/node/maxfernandez/\">Max Fernández</a>, and Maestro del Tiempo, a Nicaraguan puro, followed in 2016. Cigar Aficionado estimated Warped's output at 115,000 to 200,000 cigars a year, sold without sales reps.</p>\n<p>Production has since spread out. The map lists Corto Maduro at <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a> in Estelí. In the Dominican Republic, Warped works with <a href=\"/node/laisla/\">Tabacalera La iSLA</a>, which made Nicotina in 2022, Warped's second Dominican cigar. That puts Warped among the same peer brands as <a href=\"/node/illusione/\">Illusione</a> and <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a>, small labels that rely on a few trusted factories.</p>",
  "joya": "<p><strong>Joya de Nicaragua</strong> is Nicaragua's oldest cigar brand. On February 29, 1968, the Cuban exiles Juan Francisco Bermejo and Simón Camacho founded the Nicaragua Cigar Co. in central Estelí, the country's first cigar manufacturer. Joya de Nicaragua was at first just the name of one of its cigars. The pair had grown Cuban-seed tobacco in Honduras and then in the volcanic soil of Estelí, Condega and Jalapa. Cigar Aficionado says that by 1971 Joya was the official cigar at the White House.</p>\n<p>That success drew the attention of the dictator Anastasio Somoza, who took a majority stake and then full control. By the late 1970s the factory made more than nine million cigars a year. Because of its ties to Somoza it was destroyed in the revolution, though it was rebuilt within four months of the Sandinista victory in 1979. During the U.S. embargo of the 1980s Joya was made in Honduras by <a href=\"/node/nestorplasencia/\">Nestor Plasencia</a>. In 1994 <a href=\"/node/alejandromartinez/\">Alejandro Martínez Cuenca</a> took over the company, renamed it Tabacos Puros de Nicaragua, and set out to win back the rights to the Joya name.</p>\n<p>House lines include Antaño, Clásico, Cinco Décadas and Número Uno. The company says it sells in more than 60 markets. The <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya factory</a> also makes cigars for <a href=\"/node/dunbarton/\">Dunbarton</a>'s <a href=\"/node/sobremesa/\">Sobremesa</a>, <a href=\"/node/fratello/\">Fratello</a> and, at times, <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a>. Since January 2026 Joya has been distributed in the U.S. by SAG, its joint venture with Quesada, after about 17 years with Drew Estate.</p>",
  "laranja": "<p><strong>Laranja Reserva</strong> is made at <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a>' own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. It is named for its orange-hued Brazilian wrapper (laranja is Portuguese for orange), over Nicaraguan binder and filler. Released in 2014, it was the company's breakthrough cigar.</p>\n<p>The family now has four blends: the original Laranja, Laranja Escuro (Brazilian Mata Fina wrapper), Laranja Azulejo (Brazilian Arapiraca binder) and Laranja Hybrid. The Hybrid debuted at PCA 2026 with an Ecuadorian Habano Oscuro wrapper and Brazilian tobacco moved into the filler.</p>\n<p>Laranja is the clearest example of a cigar La Zona kept. When <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> moved to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>'s San Lotano factory in Ocotal at the end of 2016, La Zona had outgrown its building. Its director of operations, Hector Alfonso Sr., called the factory \"a modern day landmark in Estelí\" and said it would remain \"home to Laranja Reserva which was our breakthrough cigar back in 2014.\" <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> later told Cigar Aficionado that La Zona makes <a href=\"/node/crema/\">Crema</a>, <a href=\"/node/espinosahabano/\">Espinosa Habano</a> and Laranja.</p>\n<p>Laranja depends on one particular Brazilian wrapper. When COVID hit Brazil's growers, Espinosa made no Laranja for more than two years, then resumed production with the same leaf rather than switching.</p>",
  "knucklesandwich": "<p><strong>Knuckle Sandwich</strong> is the cigar brand of chef and television host <a href=\"/node/guyfieri/\">Guy Fieri</a>, developed with <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> over about 18 months and launched in 2022. <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a> handles sales and distribution. The debut came in two blends: Habano, with an Ecuador Habano wrapper, and Maduro, with an Ecuador Sumatra wrapper, both over Nicaraguan binder and filler. A Connecticut followed, and Puro Nicaragua (2025) made it four lines.</p>\n<p>Every Knuckle Sandwich is made under contract by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> at his <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal, Nicaragua, not at Espinosa's own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a>. That is the same factory that took over most <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> production in 2016. Limited editions such as the Chef Special 2025 come from San Lotano too.</p>\n<p>In June 2026 Espinosa revealed Fieri as a partner in a new factory in Santiago's free trade zone in the Dominican Republic. The third equal partner is Isaias Santana Diaz of D'Crossier and Pure Aroma Cigars. The plan called for a 30,000-square-foot building to open on September 1, 2026, starting with 25 pairs of rollers and room for 70. The partners said existing Knuckle Sandwich production would stay with A.J. Fernández in Nicaragua, and they teased a Knuckle Sandwich Dominican for the new factory.</p>",
  "sixzeroone": "<p><strong>601</strong> is a line of full-bodied Nicaraguan cigars from <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a>. It began at <a href=\"/node/eobrands/\">EO Brands</a>, the company <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> and <a href=\"/node/eddieortega/\">Eddie Ortega</a> founded in 2003. In the EO years 601 was made at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a>, the García family's operation. The line grew into color-coded labels, including Red Label Habano, Blue Maduro and Green Oscuro, plus the stronger La Bomba and Warhead releases.</p>\n<p>When EO Brands split in 2012, Espinosa kept 601, along with <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> and Mi Barrio. Production left My Father that year. It moved partly to Espinosa's new <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory in Estelí and partly to <a href=\"/node/tavicusa/\">TAVICUSA</a>, Rocky Patel's Estelí factory.</p>\n<p>La Zona soon ran out of room. In December 2016 Espinosa said it was moving most 601 production to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>'s new <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal, about 50 miles north of Estelí near the Honduran border. \"Due to the constraints placed on a small operation,\" La Zona's Hector Alfonso Sr. explained, \"we have already outgrown it.\" La Zona kept making Espinosa's other brands, including <a href=\"/node/laranja/\">Laranja Reserva</a>, <a href=\"/node/espinosahabano/\">Espinosa Habano</a> and <a href=\"/node/crema/\">Crema</a>. 601 is now made by A.J. Fernández, the same maker behind Espinosa's <a href=\"/node/knucklesandwich/\">Knuckle Sandwich</a> collaboration with Guy Fieri.</p>",
  "murcielago": "<p><strong>Murciélago</strong> (Spanish for \"bat\") is a line from <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a> known for pairing a Mexican San Andrés wrapper with Nicaraguan binder and filler, a combination <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> embraced well before it became common. It debuted at the 2009 trade show as an <a href=\"/node/eobrands/\">EO Brands</a> cigar, from the partnership Espinosa and <a href=\"/node/eddieortega/\">Eddie Ortega</a> founded in 2003. It was originally rolled at the García family's <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a> factory in Estelí.</p>\n<p>When EO Brands dissolved in 2012, Espinosa kept the name but not the blend, which stayed with the Garcías. Production moved to his own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory, with some early work at <a href=\"/node/tavicusa/\">TAVICUSA</a>. In 2015 he relaunched a reverse-engineered Murciélago with help from Amilcar Perez-Castro, a former My Father factory manager.</p>\n<p>La Zona was small, and by late 2016 Espinosa said the factory had outgrown its building. He moved most <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> production to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>'s new <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal, about 50 miles north of Estelí. Since 2017 Murciélago has been made there too. Erik Espinosa has called it one of his best sellers, running neck and neck with 601 Blue. La Zona's Hector Alfonso Sr. explained the 2016 move this way: \"In all honesty we have already outgrown it, but will never part with it.\" Since then La Zona has focused on Laranja Reserva, Espinosa Habano and Crema.</p>",
  "padron1964": "<p><strong>Padrón 1964 Anniversary</strong> is the middle and most widely celebrated prestige tier of the <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón</a> family, named for the year <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a> founded the company. Introduced as a higher-aged, more refined expression than the regular numbered line, 1964 uses extensively matured Nicaraguan tobacco (often cited in the multi-year range before rolling) and is typically box-pressed in a range of vitolas with Natural and Maduro wrappers. The result is the signature Padrón balance: cocoa, earth, coffee, and cedar-like depth without rough edges, delivered with the construction consistency that made the house a critic and retailer favorite.</p>\n<p>All 1964 cigars are made at the family's <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a> operation in Estelí under the same vertical model as the rest of the portfolio. There is no separate contract factory story and no corporate conglomerate owner. Positioning sits clearly between the approachable regular line and ultra-prestige <a href=\"/node/padron1926/\">Serie 1926</a>, which is why 1964 is often the first \"step-up\" Padrón recommended to smokers leaving milder brands.</p>\n<p>On the map, 1964 is a series node of Padrón, not an independent company. Pair it with the parent brand and 1926 when explaining the three-tier ladder, and with José Orlando's biography for why the year 1964 appears on the band. For SEO, \"Padrón 1964 vs 1926\" and \"Padrón 1964 maduro\" are high-intent queries this page should answer with factory and aging facts.</p>",
  "padron1926": "<p><strong>Padrón Serie 1926</strong> is the top tier of the <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón</a> lineup, named for the 1926 birth year of founder <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a>. It was introduced in 2001, on his 75th birthday, as an even pricier follow-up to the <a href=\"/node/padron1964/\">1964 Anniversary Series</a>, which had made Padrón famous nationally.</p>\n<p>The series quickly became one of the most decorated in the industry. A 40th anniversary Serie 1926, released in 2004 for the company's 40 years, became Cigar Aficionado's first Cigar of the Year. Padrón has had three Cigars of the Year in all, which the magazine called an unprecedented feat. Serie 1926 comes in Natural and Maduro wrappers in a short list of sizes, including the No. 9, plus anniversary editions such as the 80 Years.</p>\n<p>Like every Padrón, Serie 1926 is made by hand in Nicaragua at <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a> in Estelí, from Cuban-seed, sun-grown tobacco. The family grows much of its own leaf and ferments it in pilones for a year or more. It treats its aged inventory as precious, and the Family Reserve line uses tobacco up to ten years old. The company is now run by <a href=\"/node/jorgepadron/\">Jorge Padrón</a>, who has said everything the company does \"revolves around the philosophy my dad started.\" The family has added cigars to the portfolio slowly, and Serie 1926 sits at its top.</p>",
  "davidoff": "<p><strong>Davidoff</strong> cigars are made in the Dominican Republic, at <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a>'s <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabadom</a> factories in Villa González near Santiago. Every Davidoff cigar has been made there since 1991. The brand is owned by Oettinger Davidoff AG of Basel, Switzerland, and named for Zino Davidoff, the Geneva tobacco merchant who created it in Cuba.</p>\n<p>The first cigars carrying the Davidoff name came out of Havana's El Laguito factory in 1968. In 1970 Oettinger's Dr. Ernst Schneider took over Zino Davidoff's Geneva shop and the rights to the Davidoff trademark. The Cuban relationship broke down over quality and ownership. In August 1989 Zino publicly burned more than 100,000 cigars he judged unfit to sell, and Cuban Davidoff production ended.</p>\n<p>Schneider had found a partner in the Dominican Republic: Hendrik \"Henke\" Kelner, whose Tabadom already made <a href=\"/node/griffins/\">The Griffin's</a> and <a href=\"/node/avo/\">AVO</a>. The first Dominican Davidoffs launched in 1991, keeping the Cuban formats; the Château series became Grand Cru. Kelner oversaw Davidoff blending for three decades.</p>\n<p>The catalog now includes Grand Cru, Millennium, Aniversario, Winston Churchill and its darker Late Hour. Davidoff Nicaragua (2013) was the brand's first Nicaraguan puro, made from Nicaraguan tobacco but rolled in the Dominican Republic. Escurio (2015) uses Brazilian tobacco. Oettinger's other cigar brands include <a href=\"/node/zino/\">Zino</a> and <a href=\"/node/camacho/\">Camacho</a>.</p>",
  "arturo": "<p><strong>Arturo Fuente</strong> is one of the most respected family-owned cigar houses in the world, built across generations from a 1912 founding story into a Dominican powerhouse headquartered around Santiago. Under the leadership of the Fuente family, especially Carlos Fuente Sr. and <a href=\"/node/carlitofuente/\">Carlos \"Carlito\" Fuente Jr.</a>, the company became synonymous with Cuban-seed tobacco grown outside Cuba, obsessive aging, and lines that define entire categories: Hemingway, Don Carlos, Flor Fina 8-5-8, Añejo, Gran Reserva / Reserva, and the ultra-rare <a href=\"/node/opusx/\">Fuente Fuente OpusX</a>. House production is centered at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a>, with agricultural crown jewel Château de la Fuente supplying the estate-grown wrappers that made OpusX possible.</p>\n<p>Fuente is also a partnership engine. Longtime ally <a href=\"/node/jcnewman/\">J.C. Newman</a> of Tampa rolls Diamond Crown, MAXIMUS, Julius Caeser, Black Diamond, and related Newman marques on Fuente floors after a relationship that began in Tampa in the mid-1980s (La Unica was an early Newman brand rolled by Fuente). <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a>, owned by the Levin family, has relied on Fuente construction for its classic Dominican portfolio while building separate Nicaraguan sister brands elsewhere. Those contract relationships matter on the map: they show how one family factory can host outside trademarks without surrendering control of OpusX-level agricultural secrets.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Arturo Fuente is the Dominican anchor of traditional ultra-premium craft. Follow edges to Tabacalera A. Fuente for manufacturing, to OpusX for the estate-wrapper breakthrough, and to Newman and Ashton for the two most important outside brands that share the floor. When people search \"best Dominican cigars\" or \"Fuente vs Padron,\" this node is the factual hub: family ownership, Santiago production, Château agriculture, and a partnership web that still shapes American retail.</p>",
  "opusx": "<p><strong>Fuente Fuente OpusX</strong> is the flagship ultra-premium line of <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Arturo Fuente</a>, created by <a href=\"/node/carlitofuente/\">Carlos \"Carlito\" Fuente Jr.</a> Developed through the mid-1990s and released in November 1995 as the first commercially successful cigar to use a Cuban-seed wrapper grown in the Dominican Republic, OpusX proved that estate-level wrapper culture was possible outside Cuba. The leaf comes from the Fuentes' <strong>Château de la Fuente</strong> project, with rolling and finishing tied to <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a> in Santiago. For collectors, the double-band presentation, tiny annual volumes, and multi-year aging created a secondary market and a \"holy grail\" reputation that few brands have ever matched.</p>\n<p>OpusX is not a Nicaraguan boutique story; it is a Dominican agricultural triumph. Related Fuente programs (Perfecxion, Forbidden X, Lost City, and special anniversaries) extend the estate language without diluting the core myth. The blend is typically full, complex, and built for long aging, with power balanced by the refinement expected of Fuente construction. Trade and critic culture still treat first-growth OpusX releases as events, which keeps search demand high year after year.</p>\n<p>On the map, OpusX is a child brand of Arturo Fuente and a product of Tabacalera A. Fuente / Château agriculture. Pair this page with the parent Arturo node and the factory node when explaining why Dominican ultra-premium still competes with Estelí's boutique boom. For SEO and clarity: OpusX is Fuente family, Dominican-grown Cuban-seed wrapper, limited supply, not a corporate heritage name and not a Cuban Habanos product.</p>",
  "sancristobal": "<p><strong>San Cristóbal</strong> is an <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a> brand, owned by the Levin family's Ashton Distributors in Philadelphia. It launched in 2007 as Ashton's first Nicaraguan cigar and is made by the García family at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a> in Estelí. That makes it a sibling to Ashton's other García-made brand, <a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a>, and part of the same partnership between the Levins and <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a>.</p>\n<p>The line has grown into a family of blends that run from smooth to strong. The original San Cristóbal is full-bodied. Elegancia is the mellow member of the family. Revelation was announced in 2012 and became the fourth blend in 2013. Ovation came in 2015 as a limited edition built on long-aged tobacco, and the Ovation Decadence followed in 2016. Quintessence, a medium-to-full cigar with an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper, entered regular production in 2016. Of the World rounds out the portfolio.</p>\n<p>Like <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a> and <a href=\"/node/latelier/\">L'Atelier</a>, San Cristóbal is one of the outside brands that made the García factory known for contract work. It is rolled in the same Estelí factory as the Garcías' own My Father lines. An earlier version of this map also recorded a \"family connection\" between San Cristóbal and Tatuaje through Janny García. We found no source for it, so it has been removed.</p>",
  "ashton": "<p><strong>Ashton</strong> cigars are made by the Fuente family at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a> in the Dominican Republic, and blended with Carlito Fuente. The brand belongs to Philadelphia's Levin family, the owners of Holt's Cigar Company, and is run today by Robert Levin and his son Sathya.</p>\n<p>Albert and Jean Levin bought Holt's in 1957, and Robert took over in 1974. He began working on his own brand in 1983 and introduced Ashton at the 1985 Retail Tobacco Dealers of America convention in Washington. It came in six sizes: 8-9-8, Churchill, Corona, Panatela, Prime Minister and Sovereign. The first Ashtons were made at Tabadom. Levin had known the Fuentes all his life, and Ashton Cabinet (around 1987) and Aged Maduro were made at the Fuente factory. In 1989 he and Carlito reblended the core Ashton, and all production moved to the Fuentes. \"Once the Fuentes started making it, that's when Ashton really started to take off,\" Levin said.</p>\n<p>Ashton VSG (Virgin Sun Grown) followed in 1999, a full-bodied cigar on a Sumatra-seed wrapper that the Fuentes fermented again and again. ESG, from the Fuentes' <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Chateau de la Fuente</a> estate, marked the 20th anniversary in 2005. The Levins' Nicaraguan brands come from a different factory: <a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a> and <a href=\"/node/sancristobal/\">San Cristóbal</a> are made by the García family at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a>.</p>",
  "rockypatel": "<p><strong>Rocky Patel Premium Cigars</strong> is the company of <a href=\"/node/rakeshpatel/\">Rakesh \"Rocky\" Patel</a>, a former Los Angeles entertainment and product-liability lawyer. He got into cigars through a friend and became a founding member of the Grand Havana club in Los Angeles. In the mid-1990s he invested in a startup, Indian Tabac Cigar Co., whose first cigars were rolled in Honduras. Cigar Aficionado says Patel began selling cigars in 1995. Indian Tabac's big debut came at the 1996 RTDA trade show in Cincinnati.</p>\n<p>Patel moved from silent partner to running the business, and he worked to control production from tobacco growing to rolling. In 2003 (2002 by Cigar Aficionado's count) he renamed the company Rocky Patel Premium Cigars, and the Vintage series became the flagship. The Edge followed in 2004, sold as 100 unbanded cigars in a rough wooden box. Later came Decade, Fifteenth Anniversary and Sun Grown Maduro, which was Cigar Aficionado's No. 2 cigar of 2016.</p>\n<p>In 2005 the company opened its own factory, <a href=\"/node/tavicusa/\">TaviCusa</a> (Tabacalera Villa Cuba) in Estelí. Much of its Honduran production is made by the Plasencia family, whose factories Cigar Journal credited in 2013 with \"many of the best Rocky Patel\" cigars; see <a href=\"/node/plasenciahonduras/\">El Paraíso</a> on this map. Rocky's brother <a href=\"/node/nishpatel/\">Nish Patel</a> is executive vice president. The company is headquartered in Naples, Florida.</p>",
  "illusione": "<p><strong>Illusione Cigars</strong> is the brand of <a href=\"/node/diongiolito/\">Dion Giolito</a>. It is known for understated bands, careful blending and Nicaraguan cigars meant to age well. Its lines include Original Documents, Epernay, Rothchildes, Fume d'Amour, Haut 10, Garagiste and Singularé.</p>\n<p>Illusione's longest factory relationship is with <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> in Estelí, the factory of <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a>. That puts it alongside other brands built on Aganorsa tobacco. Other Illusione cigars come from elsewhere. The Epernay, Cruzado and ULTRA lines are made at <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> in Honduras. The Original Documents Habano was made at A.J. Fernández's <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal, which halfwheel reported in 2023 was run by Alberto Padilla. An earlier version of this page also placed Illusione at Joya de Nicaragua. We found no source for that, so it has been removed.</p>\n<p>Illusione does not make infused cigars or celebrity collaborations. Its reputation rests on the tobacco. On Cigar Nexus, the TABSA link is its main factory link, and the brand is a good contrast with boutiques built around <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a>, such as <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>. It shows how a U.S. brand owner can use several Central American factories while keeping one recognizable style.</p>",
  "viaje": "<p><strong>Viaje Cigars</strong> is Andre Farkas's small-batch boutique brand, founded around 2007–2008. It owns no factory. Most releases are made at Aganorsa Leaf's <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua, and others have come from Joya de Nicaragua, Raíces Cubanas, PDR and La iSLA.</p>\n<p>In 2019 Viaje's only regular-production line was Viaje Exclusivo, and everything else came out in limited batches throughout the year. Its best-known series is Zombie, first released in 2011. Summerfest is a recurring limited release; the 2025 Collector's Edition was three 6¼ x 54 shaggy-foot torpedoes, rolled at Aganorsa and limited to 300 boxes of 25 per blend.</p>\n<p>Viaje has worked with factories in at least four countries. The first cigars came from <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a> in 2007. In 2009 Viaje moved to <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> in Danlí, Honduras, which made Casa Fernández (now <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a>) cigars. Casa Fernández Miami and TABSA joined later, and by 2019 <a href=\"/node/andrefarkas/\">Farkas</a> said most releases were back at TABSA.</p>\n<p>From 2015 Viaje added Dominican factories. <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR Cigars</a> made the first Viaje cigars without Aganorsa tobacco, including TNT and C4, and <a href=\"/node/laisla/\">Tabacalera La iSLA</a> made New Beginnings and Mofongo. The Craft Series release Roots went back to Joya. Earlier versions of this map listed <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> as a Viaje factory; we found no source for that, so the link has been removed.</p>",
  "camacho": "<p><strong>Camacho</strong> is the Honduran brand owned by <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a>. Cuban exile Simon Camacho opened a factory in Miami in <strong>1961</strong>. After he died, Caribe Imported Cigars and the <a href=\"/node/eiroafamily/\">Eiroa family</a> took the company. Oettinger dates that purchase to <strong>1994</strong>. <a href=\"/node/christianeiroa/\">Christian Eiroa</a> has said 1995. The Camacho that smokers know was built after that, in Honduras. Julio Eiroa grew Authentic Corojo in the Jamastran Valley, from the seed line Daniel Rodríguez brought to Honduras from Cuba's El Corojo farm. Christian ran Caribe in Miami and, around 2000, put Camacho Corojo on the shelf. Rolling was at Tabacos Rancho Jamastran in Danlí, where the family also made Baccarat and La Fontana. Diploma was the upper-priming line from those Corojo years.</p>\n<p>The sale that split the story closed on <strong>1 October 2008</strong> and was announced on 13 October. Oettinger bought the company and the Rancho Jamastran factory. The farms stayed with the Eiroas. Christian, then 36, stayed president and reported to chief executive Reto Cina. Julio left the company and kept growing in the valley. A ten-year tobacco purchase agreement covered Eiroa leaf and was not renewed. Christian had founded <a href=\"/node/cle/\">CLE Cigar Company</a> by May 2012. CLE's first cigars were rolled at Rancho Jamastran, by then a Davidoff factory. CLE and Aladino now roll at the family's own <a href=\"/node/eiroadanli/\">Danlí factory</a>. That floor is not where Camacho is made.</p>\n<p>In 2013 the brand reset the catalog and the packaging, and the scorpion became the mark. Davidoff then built Diadema Cigars de Honduras on the outskirts of Danlí. The plant was announced in <strong>2015</strong>, rolling was underway in 2016, and it was the working Camacho factory by 2017. In 2015 the group also bought Finca La Merced, an 81-acre farm about 45 minutes from Danlí, to grow its own criollo and corojo. That farm does not make every Camacho an estate puro. Camacho is not rolled at <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabadom</a>. Corojo is still the power line. Connecticut is the milder line. Triple Maduro is the darker, sweeter line. Ecuador and Broadleaf sit beside them on the shelf. <strong>Liberty</strong> began in 2002 as a Fourth of July release, first in five sizes. Most years since have been one vitola, often the 11/18 figurado named for Christian Eiroa's mother's birthday on November 18. American Barrel Aged, Coyolar, Criollo, and Nicaragua Barrel Aged left the U.S. price list in October 2022.</p>\n<p>Who owns Camacho? Oettinger Davidoff, the same Swiss parent as <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a>, <a href=\"/node/avo/\">AVO</a>, <a href=\"/node/griffins/\">The Griffin's</a>, and <a href=\"/node/zino/\">Zino</a>. Davidoff, AVO, and Griffin's are the Dominican sisters at Tabadom. Zino is the other Central American brand in the group. Camacho is the Honduran one, on Davidoff's own Danlí floor. It is not an STG or Forged marca, and it is not a CLE cigar.</p>",
  "laaromadecuba": "<p><strong>La Aroma de Cuba</strong> is made by the García family at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua, for <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a>, the company of Philadelphia's Levin family. It is an old Cuban brand name that Ashton revived for the U.S. market in the early 2000s, and it is not the same product as any Cuban Habanos cigar.</p>\n<p>The first version was made in Honduras. Ashton founder Robert Levin told Cigar Aficionado he hoped to make it \"another million-unit brand.\" It became Nicaraguan through the Garcías. In 2008 Ashton introduced La Aroma de Cuba Edición Especial, made by <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a>'s family with an Ecuadorian Cuban-seed wrapper and Nicaraguan tobacco. In 2009 the original line moved to the Garcías too and was reblended.</p>\n<p>Later lines include Mi Amor (2011), the limited Noblesse (2014) and Pasión (2021). Ashton is run by Robert Levin and his son Sathya, and its core cigars are made in the Dominican Republic at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a>, so La Aroma de Cuba gives the company a fuller Nicaraguan side. Ashton's <a href=\"/node/sancristobal/\">San Cristóbal</a>, launched in 2007, was its first García-made brand. Cigar Aficionado reported in 2008 that San Cristóbal was a \"major client\" for García, alongside <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a> and 601.</p>",
  "alecbradley": "<p><strong>Alec Bradley</strong> cigars are made mainly in Honduras, at the Endemaño family's <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> factory in Danlí. Since 2023 the brand has been owned by <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, which also owns <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>. Alan Rubin founded the company in 1996 after selling his family's hardware and fasteners business, and named it for his sons Alec and Bradley.</p>\n<p>Its broad catalog includes Prensado, Black Market, Tempus, Magic Toast, Kintsugi and American Classic. The Prensado Churchill was Cigar Aficionado's No. 1 Cigar of the Year for 2011, rated 96 points.</p>\n<p>Rubin ran a blending and marketing house that used partner factories rather than owning one. Raíces Cubanas, founded in Danlí in May 2003 by Romay, Maria and Hugo Endemaño, became the main one. In 2013 Alec Bradley even released a cigar named for the factory, Raíces Cubanas 1941, after the year Romay was born. Ralph Montero, Alec Bradley's executive vice-president and partner, spent two decades with the company and was credited with running its products. Esteban Disla, later of Domain, has said he created Alec Bradley blends during his years at Latin Cigars in Estelí.</p>\n<p>After the 2023 sale, Montero worked briefly with STG, then in late 2024 rejoined the Endemaños as Raíces Cubanas launched its own four-line portfolio. The brand is now sold through STG's <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged</a> unit.</p>",
  "lapalina": "<p>La Palina is one of the most historically significant American cigar marcas, tracing to <strong>1896</strong> when Ukrainian émigré Samuel Paley founded the <strong>Congress Cigar Factory</strong> in Chicago, the city that was then home to hundreds of cigar operations. Paley named his first cigar range <strong>La Palina</strong> in honor of his wife, <strong>Goldie Drell Paley</strong>; at its height Congress rolled roughly <strong>one million cigars per day</strong>. The Great Depression eventually closed the factory, but not before Paley family capital helped launch what became <strong>CBS</strong> under Sam's son William S. Paley, linking America's broadcast era to one of its great Gilded Age cigarmakers.</p>\n<p>William \"Bill\" Paley, Sam's grandson, revived the brand in <strong>2010</strong> on a New York rooftop as a personal luxury cigar project, after an early, short-lived collaboration with Graycliff in the Bahamas tied to the family's Lyford Cay estate. La Palina 2.0 is a <strong>negociant</strong>: it does not own a factory, but it does own its tobacco, finished products, and brands. Five years after relaunch, Bill partnered with veterans <strong>Samuel \"Sammy\" Phillips</strong> (Rocky Patel, Alec Bradley) and <strong>Clay Roberts</strong> (Alec Bradley, A.J. Fernandez); today they steward operations while Bill remains chairman. Portfolio cigars are crafted in <strong>Honduras</strong>, <strong>Nicaragua</strong> with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a>, the <strong>Dominican Republic</strong> (including <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR Cigars</a>), and <strong>Miami</strong>: where the limited-production <strong>Goldie</strong> line is rolled at <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a>. Current releases span Goldie, Mr. Sam, Classic, White/Black/Blue/Bronze/Silver Label, Family Series Miami, La Palina 1948, and the <strong>125 Anos</strong> anniversary edition.</p>",
  "westtampa": "<p><strong>West Tampa Tobacco Co.</strong> was announced in May 2022 by <a href=\"/node/rickrodriguez/\">Rick Rodriguez</a>, days after he retired from STG/General Cigar. He had been there almost 25 years and was best known as the blender for <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>. Rodriguez is master blender and co-founder. His partner is Gus Martinez, a former colleague, who is president and co-founder.</p>\n<p>The company is named for Tampa's old cigar district, and the box lid tells the story of Rodriguez's family. It launched with two regular-production blends, White and Black, both made at <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia Cigar Co.</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua. White has a Habano wrapper over Nicaraguan filler. Black uses the same box and band design in a darker presentation. Cigar Aficionado later reported the addition of Red.</p>\n<p>Not everything comes from Garmendia. Cigar Coop reported that later releases, including Boliche Blvd (2023) and Dark Time (2025), were made at <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a> in Estelí, a factory unrelated to Oliva Cigar Co.</p>\n<p>West Tampa is independent of <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>. Rodriguez's career connects the two on Cigar Nexus, but there is no ownership link. An earlier version of this page described a three-decade career and a family role for a daughter. Neither is in the sources, so both have been removed.</p>",
  "avo": "<p><strong>AVO</strong> is the premium Dominican brand born from the friendship of Armenian-American jazz pianist and composer <strong>Avo Uvezian</strong> and master blender <strong>Hendrik \"Henke\" Kelner</strong>. Uvezian, already a celebrated musician who had spent years in Puerto Rico and the Caribbean, set out in the 1980s to make a cigar that matched his ear for composition. He was introduced to Kelner, then building what would become <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabacos Dominicanos (Tabadom)</a> in Villa González. Their first commercial year produced a small run (Kelner later recalled roughly 17,000 cigars that Uvezian carried out under his arms). The Davidoff store on Madison Avenue began carrying the brand in <strong>1988</strong>, and by the mid-1990s U.S. demand had soared. After the launch of AVO Maduro, Oettinger approached Uvezian about purchasing the marca; the deal kept his name on the band and left him active with the brand. AVO has been part of the <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a> portfolio ever since, while Uvezian's musical persona (he died in 2017 at 91) still shapes packaging, limited editions, and the brand's annual LE tradition.</p>\n<p>Production remains at Tabadom under Swiss group quality systems, the same Dominican floor that also rolls much of the <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a> and <a href=\"/node/griffins/\">Griffin's</a> portfolios. Core lines emphasize balance over raw power. <strong>Classic</strong> is the mild-to-medium gateway (often an Ecuador Connecticut wrapper over Dominican long fillers), with a parallel <strong>Classic Maduro</strong> for darker sweetness. <strong>XO</strong> steps up with longer-aged Dominican filler and binder under a high-grade Ecuador Connecticut leaf, frequently cited for six-year aged components and a creamy, woody profile. <strong>Heritage</strong> brings Ecuador sun-grown wrapper character and a warmer medium body. The <strong>Syncro</strong> family (including Syncro Nicaragua and Fogata) deliberately folds Nicaraguan leaf into the Dominican recipe for a more modern, fuller edge without abandoning AVO's polished identity. Seasonal and anniversary projects (Improvisation, Domaine, Signature and Maduro anniversary releases, and the annual limited editions Uvezian pioneered) keep the catalog in motion for collectors.</p>\n<p>On the map, AVO is how a musician's personal project became a permanent Oettinger luxury node: not a boutique Estelí startup, but a Dominican-made, Swiss-owned brand that still sells the idea of jazz composition in every band name. It sits between approachable Griffin's and flagship Davidoff in the same corporate/manufacturing cluster, proof that Tabadom can host both gateway and luxury SKUs under one roof. For enthusiasts following ownership trees, AVO is owned by Oettinger, made at Tabadom, and historically inseparable from Kelner's Dominican factory story, even after the factory itself passed fully into Davidoff-group hands.</p>",
  "griffins": "<p><strong>The Griffin's</strong> is a Dominican cigar brand owned by <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a>. It began in 1984 as a house cigar for The Griffin's Club, a Geneva nightclub owned by Bernard Grobet. The cigars were made in the Dominican Republic by Hendrik \"Henke\" Kelner, then running cigar production at Tabacalera S.A., which made Cacique and Vega Real for the U.S. and The Griffin's for Europe.</p>\n<p>That same year Kelner left to found Tabacos Dominicanos, better known as <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabadom</a>. He took The Griffin's with him and expanded it to six sizes. Grobet's guests liked the cigars enough that they were soon sold in Swiss tobacconists and, from 1989, in the United States. Grobet first used Davidoff of Geneva in Stamford, Connecticut, to handle exports, then sold the brand to the Oettinger Davidoff group in Basel in 1992.</p>\n<p>The Griffin's also helped make Davidoff Dominican. Grobet introduced Kelner to Davidoff owner Dr. Ernst Schneider in 1988, and Schneider, already distributing The Griffin's and <a href=\"/node/avo/\">AVO</a>, moved <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a> production out of Cuba. The first Dominican Davidoffs were made at Tabadom in 1990. Within the Tabadom group, the smaller brands, including AVO, The Griffin's and <a href=\"/node/zino/\">Zino</a>, have been made by its OK Cigar unit. For the brand's 25th anniversary in 2009, Davidoff released a Special XXV Edition.</p>",
  "zino": "<p><strong>Zino</strong> is the <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a> brand named for Zino Davidoff. The company says it was founded in Honduras in 1978, when Dr. Ernst Schneider and Zino Davidoff created it for the U.S. market at the height of the Cuban embargo. Honduras, with its tobacco tradition centered on the Jamastrán Valley, became the brand's first home. Santa Rosa's <a href=\"/node/laflordecopan/\">La Flor de Copán</a> factory lists Zino among the cigars it made in the past.</p>\n<p>As <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a> moved its flagship production to the Dominican Republic around 1990, the original Zino faded into the background. Within the Dominican <a href=\"/node/tabadom/\">Tabadom</a> group, Zino was among the smaller brands made by the OK Cigar unit, alongside <a href=\"/node/avo/\">AVO</a> and <a href=\"/node/griffins/\">The Griffin's</a>.</p>\n<p>The name came back in 2002 with <strong>Zino Platinum</strong>, a high-priced line marketed to the hip-hop world with the help of Peter Arnell and Steve Stoute. Its first Crown Series cost about $40 a cigar. The Scepter Series followed with five shorter sizes and a revised blend meant to deliver a similar experience in less time. In 2021 Davidoff relaunched the brand again as <strong>Zino Nicaragua</strong>, a spicy, full-bodied cigar using tobacco from Estelí and Jalapa. <strong>Zino Honduras</strong> followed on June 26, 2026. It has an Ecuadorian wrapper over tobacco from Copán and the Jamastrán Valley, is rolled at Diadema Cigars de Honduras in Danlí, and comes with a pre-cut head on some packs.</p>",
  "macanudo": "<p><strong>Macanudo</strong> is one of the best-selling premium cigar brands in the United States, produced and distributed by <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> under <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. The modern brand was launched for the U.S. market in <strong>1971</strong> after General acquired Temple Hall in Kingston, Jamaica (1969) and reworked the marca with legends including Alfons Mayer and Ramón Cifuentes. Early Café blends paired Connecticut Shade wrappers (from General/Cullman shade culture and proprietary seed lines such as 211 and 8212) with Dominican, Jamaican, and Mexican tobaccos, creating the mild-to-medium \"everyday premium\" profile that defined American humidor shelves for decades.</p>\n<p>Production later consolidated in the Dominican Republic after Jamaica operations closed around <strong>2000</strong>, while the catalog expanded far beyond Café: Maduro, Gold Label / Vintage programs, Cru Royale, Inspirado, Estate Reserve, and numerous limiteds keep the brand relevant for smokers who want consistency more than boutique scarcity. Map edges also show STG Nicaraguan capacity (including <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a> and related network routing) for select SKUs, so Macanudo is no longer only a single-country mild Connecticut story. Scale, national distribution, and brand recognition remain the point: Macanudo is STG's accessible face beside bolder siblings like <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a> and <a href=\"/node/lagloriacubana/\">La Gloria Cubana</a>.</p>\n<p>For searchers asking \"is Macanudo mild,\" \"who makes Macanudo,\" or \"Macanudo vs Montecristo,\" the answers are ownership (General/STG, not Altadis), factory geography (historic Jamaica to Dominican volume plus STG network), and product ladder (Café gateway through fuller Inspirado/limited lines). On Cigar Nexus, Macanudo is the volume anchor of the STG classics cluster and a hub for internal links to General Cigar, STG, and sister heritage brands.</p>",
  "cao": "<p><strong>CAO</strong> is the bold, design-forward brand that helped pull a younger generation into full-flavored premium cigars in the 1990s and early 2000s. Founded as an independent creative house and later absorbed into <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> / <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, CAO became famous for loud packaging, country-themed lines, and blends that refused mild Dominican comfort. Core and cult expressions include Brazilia, Ecuador, Italia, Flathead, MX2, Consigliere, and Conquest-era projects, many leaning Nicaraguan and multi-origin strength rather than Connecticut mildness.</p>\n<p>The human face of CAO for nearly three decades was master blender and ambassador <a href=\"/node/rickrodriguez/\">Rick Rodriguez</a>, who later founded <a href=\"/node/westtampa/\">West Tampa Tobacco Co.</a> after leaving the corporate system. His tenure links CAO's cult years to General's distribution muscle. Production and related facilities on the map include Nicaraguan capacity and the <a href=\"/node/caoamerican/\">CAO American Caribbean</a> node for select routing, reflecting STG's multi-factory model rather than a single boutique floor. Historical blender credits on the graph also include talent such as Esteban Disla for certain projects, showing how corporate brands still pull from Estelí's freelance and factory talent pool.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, CAO is STG's creative counterweight to Macanudo's mild classic image: same parent, different smoker. Pair this page with Rick Rodriguez, West Tampa, General Cigar, and STG when tracing how an irreverent 1990s brand became a permanent corporate SKU engine without losing its loud shelf identity.</p>\n<p>CAO cigars are rolled in Estelí at Scandinavian Tobacco Group's <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>, and some lines, such as CAO Arcana Firewalker, come from <a href=\"/node/caoamerican/\">American Caribbean Cigars</a>.</p>",
  "cohiba_nc": "<p><strong>Cohiba (Non-Cuban)</strong> is the U.S.-market Cohiba controlled by <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a> and sold today primarily through <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged Cigar Co.</a> (with sister routing historically through <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>). It is <em>not</em> the Cuban Cohiba of Habanos S.A. Decades of trademark litigation left two legal systems: Cuban Cohiba for much of the world, and General/STG Cohiba for the United States and other markets where the General trademark holds. That split is the single most important fact for any shopper who sees the yellow-and-black band on a U.S. shelf.</p>\n<p>Non-Cuban Cohiba has long mixed Dominican and Nicaraguan capacity. On this map, production and portfolio routing touch <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>. Core expressions range from milder classic and Red Dot-style profiles aimed at everyday retail to fuller Blue Dot, Black, and Nicaragua-forward lines built for smokers who want power under a name they already know. Packaging, pricing, and national distribution reflect STG scale rather than boutique scarcity.</p>\n<p>For Cigar Nexus, Cohiba (NC) anchors the STG side of the Cuban-legacy trademark split. It sits with <a href=\"/node/partagas_nc/\">Partagas (NC)</a> and <a href=\"/node/punch_nc/\">Punch (NC)</a> under the Danish corporate umbrella, while <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> (Imperial Brands) holds the parallel U.S. franchises for Montecristo and Romeo y Julieta.</p>\n<p>Shoppers comparing a yellow-banded U.S. Cohiba to a Cuban box should treat them as different products with different leaf, factories, and legal owners. Non-Cuban Cohiba cannot substitute for Habanos production or aging culture; it is a parallel marca built for markets where General/STG holds the trademark. Read the band for Forged or General routing, then the box for Dominican or Nicaraguan origin, and you already know more than most retail shelves imply.</p>",
  "partagas_nc": "<p><strong>Partagas (Non-Cuban)</strong> is a <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> / <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a> heritage brand, completely separate from Habanos S.A.'s Cuban Partagás. After the Revolution, Ramón Cifuentes left Cuba rather than run the nationalized industry. In <strong>1974</strong> the Cifuentes family licensed Partagas manufacture to General Cigar; the first non-Cuban Partagas reached market in <strong>1977</strong> from Jamaica before production shifted to the Dominican Republic, long associated with Cameroon wrappers, Mexican binders, and Dominican (and later multi-origin) fillers. That Dominican identity made Partagas one of the best-selling premium names in the U.S. long before the boutique boom.</p>\n<p>Today the portfolio spans classic black-band expressions, fuller specials, and newer experiments such as Connecticut-wrapped and Nicaraguan-forward SKUs (including Cortado and other limited lines covered in the trade press). Manufacturing still lives inside the STG network rather than a single boutique floor. Recent STG U.S. portfolio shuffles have moved Partagas onto the General Cigar sales team (while <a href=\"/node/cohiba_nc/\">Cohiba</a> and <a href=\"/node/punch_nc/\">Punch</a> shifted toward Forged), so retail routing can change even when corporate ownership stays Danish.</p>\n<p>On the map, Partagas (NC) is a textbook embargo-era marca: Cuban name, American trademark path, Caribbean and Central American tobacco, STG distribution muscle. Pair it with Cohiba (NC) and Punch (NC) for the STG heritage cluster, and with <a href=\"/node/montecristonc/\">Montecristo (NC)</a> / <a href=\"/node/ryjnc/\">Romeo y Julieta (NC)</a> when explaining why two different conglomerates own different famous Cuban names in the U.S.</p><p>Partagas remains a daily medium-to-full benchmark in many U.S. humidors because the name is historic and the non-Cuban execution stayed widely available for decades. Cameroon-wrapper lore from the Dominican era still defines the brand's classic reputation, while newer SKUs chase Connecticut mildness or Nicaraguan strength without abandoning the black-and-gold identity. For map readers, Partagas is how STG keeps a Cuban-era name commercially alive with Caribbean and Central American tobacco rather than Havana leaf.</p>",
  "punch_nc": "<p><strong>Punch (non-Cuban)</strong> is made in Danlí, Honduras, at <a href=\"/node/stgdanli/\">STG Danlí (HATSA)</a>, for <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>, part of <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. Since February 2026 it has been sold by STG's <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged Cigar Co.</a> It is legally and operationally separate from the Cuban Punch sold by Habanos S.A.</p>\n<p>The U.S. brand came out of the Cuban embargo. Tampa's Villazon &amp; Co., run by Frank Llaneza and Dan Blumenthal, bought the U.S. rights to Punch and <a href=\"/node/hoyonc/\">Hoyo de Monterrey</a> from Fernando Palicio, their pre-revolution Cuban owner, around 1965. Llaneza had started HATSA in Danlí around 1964, so Punch has been Honduran from the start. By the late 1990s Villazon was making about 125,000 handmade cigars a day in Honduras, mostly Punch and Hoyo. General Cigar agreed in 1996 to buy Villazon for $91 million, and the deal closed in January 1997.</p>\n<p>Punch's lines include the classic Punch, Deluxe, Signature, Gran Puro, Diablo and Knuckle Buster. Rare Corojo, despite its name, uses an Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper over a Connecticut broadleaf binder and Honduran, Nicaraguan and Dominican filler. It was an annual limited release for decades until General made it regular production in January 2025, with nine sizes plus a box-pressed 6 x 60 El Gordo, all made at STG Danlí.</p>\n<p>An earlier version of this page called the Cuban owner \"Fernando Palacios\" and said much Punch was made at <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>. The name was wrong, and we found no source for the Estelí claim.</p>",
  "trinidadespiritu": "<p><strong>Trinidad Espiritu</strong> is made in Estelí, Nicaragua, at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a>, for <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a>, which owns the Trinidad trademark in the U.S. It is a Nicaraguan line blended by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> with <a href=\"/node/rafaelnodal/\">Rafael Nodal</a>, Tabacalera USA's head of product capability.</p>\n<p>Altadis teased it in April 2019 and announced it in May for Trinidad's 50th anniversary. The theme was the late 1960s and early 1970s, with a launch tour of Latin Caribbean music, cocktails and dominoes that kicked off in Miami in June. It was Fernández's first cigar for the brand. The first release, Series No. 1, was a Nicaraguan puro in five sizes: Robusto, Toro, Magnum, Belicoso and an event-only 7½ x 40 Fundador. Regular production shipped in July 2019, from about $9.90 to $11 a cigar.</p>\n<p>halfwheel had already marked it Series No. 1 with two more planned. Series No. 2 uses a Brazilian Arapiraca maduro wrapper. Series No. 3 uses a Mexican San Andrés wrapper, and its Belicoso was No. 5 on Cigar Aficionado's Top 25 for 2025, at 95 points. The Miami Edition (2025) was a limited 6⅛ x 52 Belicoso of 4,000 boxes. Its artwork is about Miami, but it is made in Nicaragua. An earlier version of this page placed it at Fernández's San Lotano factory in Ocotal.</p>\n<p>Nodal and Fernández also worked together on <a href=\"/node/montecristonc/\">Montecristo Nicaragua</a>, <a href=\"/node/hupmannnc/\">H. Upmann</a> by AJ Fernández and Romeo San Andrés.</p>",
  "cain": "<p><strong>Cain</strong> is made by <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a> at its <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. It is Oliva's high-ligero brand, launched at the 2009 IPCPR trade show and fronted, like <a href=\"/node/nub/\">NUB</a> a year earlier, by Sam Leccia.</p>\n<p>Cain was sold as a \"straight ligero\" cigar. Leccia said the filler was about 82 percent ligero, not 100, because an all-ligero cigar would not burn properly. The filler blends three Nicaraguan ligeros: Estelí, the strongest; Condega, medium and complex; and Jalapa, the lightest. It is triple-fermented to take the edge off, with seco and volado leaves for combustion. The first lines were Habano and Maduro, both Nicaraguan puros, sold in 24-count boxes with a 12-page pamphlet on the blend.</p>\n<p>Cain F (\"Forced Refined\") pushed further, with more Estelí ligero. Retailers first got it as a prerelease five-pack of 5 x 50 robustos in August 2009, and strong feedback made it a regular line the next spring. Sizes are named for their dimensions, such as the 550 and 660. Later lines include Black, Connecticut CT, Daytona and Cain NUB crossovers.</p>\n<p>Leccia left Oliva at the end of 2010. Jose Oliva said Cain and NUB were Oliva brands that Leccia did not own, and both moved under Oliva's Studio Tobac umbrella. Oliva itself has been owned since 2016 by J. Cortès. Its flagship <a href=\"/node/seriev/\">Serie V</a> comes from the same factory.</p>",
  "nub": "<p><strong>NUB</strong> is made by <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a> in Nicaragua, at the company's <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> factory in Estelí. It is Oliva's short, very thick cigar, launched in early 2008. No NUB is longer than four inches, and some reach 66 ring gauge, more than an inch across.</p>\n<p>The idea came from Sam Leccia, a Pittsburgh native and Oliva sales representative. Around 2006 he started working on \"a high quality cigar that developed its optimum flavor extremely quickly.\" Most cigars reach their sweet spot somewhere between 3½ and 4 inches, so NUB starts there. Leccia reportedly rolled the prototype himself. Oliva says the heavy load of tobacco makes plenty of smoke and a dense ash that keeps the cigar cool. The first three blends were Connecticut, Cameroon and Habano, and Maduro followed in 2009. Sizes are named for their dimensions, such as the 460 (4 x 60).</p>\n<p>Leccia left Oliva at the end of 2010. Jose Oliva said then that NUB and its sibling <a href=\"/node/cain/\">Cain</a>, an all-ligero brand Leccia also fronted, were Oliva brands that Leccia did not own. Both moved under Oliva's new Studio Tobac umbrella. Later NUB lines include Dub, the coffee-themed Nuance, and Cain NUB crossovers. Oliva itself has been owned since 2016 by the Belgian J. Cortès group.</p>",
  "lagloriacubana": "<p><strong>La Gloria Cubana (non-Cuban)</strong> is the full-flavored General Cigar / <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a> brand that began as a Miami Little Havana story and became a national powerhouse. The Perez-Carrillo family launched the modern U.S. marca from El Credito in the 1970s; <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a> took the blending reins and, by the early 1990s, La Gloria Cubana was a critical and cult sensation (including major <em>Cigar Aficionado</em> attention). Demand forced a move from Miami-scale production toward Dominican capacity. In <strong>1999</strong>, the brand (and El Credito story) entered the General Cigar / Swedish Match universe; Ernesto stayed about a decade as blender and ambassador before leaving in <strong>2009</strong> to found what is now <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a>.</p>\n<p>Under corporate ownership the brand kept its reputation for strength and value. <strong>Serie R</strong> helped normalize large ring gauges (including regular-production 6x60 territory) in the early 2000s, while Classic / Wavell-era blends, Maduro variants, and later lines such as Intención, Los Gloriosos, and anniversary projects keep the catalog moving. Factory routing on this map includes Dominican and STG Nicaraguan capacity (<a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a> among other edges). A 2017 Colección Reserva collaboration brought Ernesto back into a limited partnership with General, underlining that the brand's DNA still points to the Perez-Carrillo years even when legal ownership is STG.</p>\n<p>La Gloria Cubana sits in the fuller-bodied tier of the STG classics cluster opposite mild <a href=\"/node/macanudo/\">Macanudo</a> and creative <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>. Map it with Ernesto Perez-Carrillo and Casa Carrillo for the human dynasty, and with General/STG for who owns the trademark today. For SEO, disambiguate from any Cuban La Gloria Cubana Habanos product: this page is the U.S. non-Cuban marca born in Miami and scaled by General.</p>",
  "room101": "<p><strong>Room101</strong> is Matt Booth's cigar brand, owned since June 2022 by <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. Its cigars come from several factories, mainly <a href=\"/node/williamventura/\">Tabacalera William Ventura</a> in the Dominican Republic and <a href=\"/node/stgdanli/\">STG Danlí (HATSA)</a> in Honduras. Since February 2026 it has been sold by <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>.</p>\n<p>Booth, a former U.S. Marine, started Room101 in 2003 as a jewelry company making custom silver pieces. The name refers to the torture room in Orwell's <em>1984</em> and to Highway 101 in Los Angeles. Cigars came in 2009 through a partnership with <a href=\"/node/camacho/\">Camacho</a>, which Davidoff had just bought. Davidoff made the cigars in Honduras and sold them in the U.S. until Booth let the contract lapse on January 1, 2017. Namakubi (2011), an early limited line, came from Davidoff's Camacho factory.</p>\n<p>Booth returned later in 2017 with brands made with Caldwell. In 2019 he revived The Big Payback as a maduro made at HATSA, his first Honduran work since Davidoff. When STG bought the brand, Booth joined as creative director; the deal didn't include his jewelry lines. The 2025 Namakubi Chingon, a 5½ x 60 figurado, is made at William Ventura, which also makes Doomsayer.</p>\n<p>Other lines include Farce, Hit &amp; Run and Name Dropper. An earlier version of this map linked Room101 to the Eiroa family's CLE factory in Danlí. Its Honduran cigars were made at Davidoff's Camacho factory, not CLE, so that link has been removed.</p>",
  "jcnewman": "<p><strong>J.C. Newman Cigar Co.</strong> makes cigars in three places: its own El Reloj factory in Tampa, its own PENSA factory in Estelí, Nicaragua, and, for its Dominican brands, the Fuente family's <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a>. Founded in 1895 in Cleveland, it calls itself America's oldest family-owned premium cigar maker.</p>\n<p>Julius Caeser Newman was born in Austria-Hungary and learned to roll cigars as an apprentice in Cleveland. Laid off in a recession, he turned the family barn into a one-man factory on May 5, 1895. In 1953 he sent his son Stanford to Tampa, and in 1954 the company bought the Regensburg factory in Ybor City, known for its clock tower as El Reloj. It is now the last working cigar factory in Tampa.</p>\n<p>The company nearly went under in 1986, when Stanford and his sons Eric and Bobby bought out the other family shareholders. Weeks later Carlos Fuente Sr. asked Stanford to make his Tampa brand, Moya. Stanford agreed if Fuente would make a Dominican cigar for Newman. That cigar, La Unica, became the start of the <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Arturo Fuente</a> partnership, which went on to make Cuesta-Rey, <a href=\"/node/diamondcrown/\">Diamond Crown</a> (1996), MAXIMUS, Julius Caeser and Black Diamond.</p>\n<p>In 2011 Eric and Bobby built PENSA (Puros de Estelí Nicaragua) after years of Nicaraguan production at <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a>. It makes <a href=\"/node/brickhouse/\">Brick House</a>, Perla del Mar, El Baton and Quorum, and the company says 840 employees roll 100,000 cigars a day there.</p>",
  "diamondcrown": "<p><strong>Diamond Crown</strong> is the ultra-premium flagship line of <a href=\"/node/jcnewman/\">J.C. Newman</a>, handmade at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a> in the Dominican Republic. Created for Newman's <strong>100th anniversary in 1995</strong> by Stanford Newman with Carlos Fuente Jr., the original Diamond Crown Classic pursued \"the best cigar in the world\" with multi-year aged Dominican binder and filler under Connecticut Shade or Connecticut Broadleaf wrappers, tissue-wrapped presentation, and packaging as ornate as top-shelf spirits. The 54-ring formats helped popularize thicker vitolas in the mid-1990s boom. Later extensions (Maximus-related programs, special finishes, and anniversary humidors) keep the brand at the top of Newman's Dominican ladder.</p>\n<p>Recent projects such as Diamond Crown Tampa push the partnership further, including Florida sun-grown tobacco grown with retail partners and still rolled by Fuente, proof that the Newman-Fuente relationship keeps innovating rather than freezing in 1995 amber. Construction, aging discipline, and limited positioning place Diamond Crown in the same ultra-premium conversation as <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a>'s upper lines and <a href=\"/node/opusx/\">OpusX</a>, though each marca has a different ownership story.</p>\n<p>On the map, Diamond Crown is a Newman brand manufactured at Tabacalera A. Fuente, not a Fuente-owned trademark. That distinction matters for collectors tracing who owns the name versus who rolls the cigar. Pair this node with J.C. Newman for company history and Arturo Fuente / Tabafuente for the factory and agricultural context behind the smoke.</p>",
  "espinosahabano": "<p><strong>Espinosa Habano</strong> is a core line from <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a>, the company led by <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a>. It was among the first house lines made at Espinosa's own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory, which opened in 2012 in a three-story former inn in downtown Estelí. The original version was a Nicaraguan puro.</p>\n<p>In October 2015 Espinosa relaunched the line with new packaging and a revised blend: an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler, still rolled at La Zona. A 7½ x 38 Espinosa Habano Lancero followed in 2019 with the same wrapper and Nicaraguan binder and filler. La Zona's style shows in the cigar. The factory bunches entubado-style and finishes with triple caps, and Cigar Coop named it Small Factory of the Year in 2015 and 2016.</p>\n<p>The Habano matters most for where it is not made. In December 2016 Espinosa moved most <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> production to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>'s new <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal, because La Zona had run out of room, and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> followed. Espinosa Habano stayed home. La Zona's Hector Alfonso Sr. said then that the factory would keep making Espinosa's other brands, and Espinosa later told Cigar Aficionado that La Zona makes Espinosa Habano, <a href=\"/node/crema/\">Crema</a> and <a href=\"/node/laranja/\">Laranja Reserva</a>. That makes the Habano one of the clearest examples of what Espinosa's own factory produces.</p>",
  "crema": "<p><strong>Espinosa Crema</strong> is the first Connecticut-wrapped cigar to carry the Espinosa name. <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a> announced it in May 2016 and shipped it right after that summer's IPCPR show. It uses an Ecuadorian Connecticut-seed wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler. Hector Alfonso Sr., operations manager at La Zona, called it a \"gateway cigar\" for smokers new to the brand.</p>\n<p>That matters because the Espinosa name was built on strength. The company's best-known early cigars were the oscuro-wrapped <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> lines and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a>, both full-bodied. Crema was meant as the mild end of the range. It launched in three sizes (Robusto, Toro Grande and Churchill), and the line has since added more, including a corona, a rabito, a lancero and a box-pressed toro. It is a separate blend from the value-priced La Zona Connecticut. Cigar Coop reviewed the Toro Grande in October 2016, a few months after launch.</p>\n<p>Crema is rolled at Espinosa's own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> factory in Estelí, which <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> opened in 2012. Espinosa has said La Zona still makes Crema, <a href=\"/node/espinosahabano/\">Espinosa Habano</a> and <a href=\"/node/laranja/\">Laranja Reserva</a>, while 601 and Murciélago moved to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>. That makes Crema one of the house lines that still shows La Zona's own work, including its entubado bunching and triple caps.</p>",
  "sobremesa": "<p><strong>Sobremesa</strong> is the flagship of <a href=\"/node/dunbarton/\">Dunbarton Tobacco &amp; Trust</a> and the first cigar <a href=\"/node/stevesaka/\">Steve Saka</a> released after leaving <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a>, where he had been CEO. Saka resigned in 2013 and sat out a two-year non-compete. Sobremesa was the most-hyped debut at the 2015 IPCPR show and shipped on November 6, 2015.</p>\n<p>The blend uses a La Meca Ecuador Habano Grade 1 wrapper and a Mexican Matacapan Negro de temporal binder over five fillers: GK Condega C-SG seco, Pueblo Nuevo criollo viso, La Joya Estelí C-98 viso, an ASP Estelí hybrid ligero and Pennsylvania broadleaf ligero from Lancaster County. Saka says he developed the blend himself.</p>\n<p>The first shipment was 1,000 boxes across five sizes: Corona Grande, Cervantes Fino, Robusto Largo, El Americano and Gran Imperiales. Saka filled 108 accounts and put 126 more on a waiting list. Elegante en Cedros, a 7 x 50 Churchill sold in boxes of 13, followed in 2016. The Cervantes Fino was discontinued in October 2023.</p>\n<p>Sobremesa is made at <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a> in Estelí, a factory Saka knew well because Drew Estate had distributed <a href=\"/node/joya/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a>'s own brand in the U.S. In 2019 Dunbarton added <strong>Sobremesa Brûlée</strong>, a milder version with an Ecuadorian Connecticut shade wrapper.</p>",
  "belladamacigars": "<p><strong>Bella Dama Cigars</strong> is a poker-themed boutique brand from St. Petersburg, Florida, founded and run by <a href=\"/node/chantelleavitt/\">Chantel Leavitt</a>. The lines carry card-table names, such as The King, The Queen, Ace and Full House, and the lower-priced Dealers Select range was added in 2024.</p>\n<p>The brand started with a U.S. manufacturer. After sampling with several factories in 2023, Leavitt chose <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragón</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua. On August 12, 2024, Bella Dama announced that Aragón would be the sole producer of all its premium cigars. The relaunch followed on September 1, 2024. The six existing cigars stayed in the line with upgraded wrappers, binders and fillers. Dealers Select added three new cigars at a more accessible price, and the logo was refreshed to match. The company also said it would move into brick-and-mortar retail and list new shops on its website as they signed on.</p>\n<p>Bella Dama owns no factory, which is the norm for small U.S. labels. Its maker is the same Aragón family workshop that rolled <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a> from 2018 to 2024. Other Estelí-made independents on the map include <a href=\"/node/definition/\">Definition</a> and the brands made at <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a>. All of them rely on small contract factories rather than the large Estelí operations.</p>",
  "karenberger": "<p><strong>Karen Berger Cigars</strong> is the Estelí cigar company led by <a href=\"/node/karenbergerperson/\">Karen Berger</a>, widow of Enrique \"Don Kiki\" Berger. Don Kiki founded the family's factory in Nicaragua in the 1990s, and in 1995 he launched his own Don Kiki Red, Green, White and Brown labels. He also ran the Cuban Crafters Megastore in Miami's Little Havana. After he died in 2014, Karen kept the Don Kiki brand going and added blends of her own.</p>\n<p>She launched K by Karen Berger at the 2016 IPCPR show. The \"K\" honors both her and Kiki. It is a box-pressed Nicaraguan puro whose filler comes from her own farm, first offered in Habano and Maduro. In 2023 the company changed its name from K by Karen Berger to Karen Berger Cigars, keeping the logo, packaging and line names. Later releases include Tailgate (2020), a Sumatra-wrapped toro sold at a low price in 14-count boxes; K-Fire 150°F, named for the peak temperature in a fermentation pile; and Ixtelli and Halftime, presented at InterTabac. The company says it sells in 17 countries.</p>\n<p>The cigars are made at the family's own <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraesteli/\">Tabacalera Estelí</a>, now housed in a former hotel in central Estelí. The exception was the 2022 K by Karen Berger 25th Anniversary, made by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>, a longtime friend of Kiki's who also supplies Karen's wrappers. She also runs the Don Kiki Cigars Superstore in Daytona Beach, Florida.</p>",
  "domain": "<p><strong>Domain Cigars</strong> is a boutique brand co-founded by <a href=\"/node/estebandisla/\">Esteban Disla</a> and <a href=\"/node/daniellance/\">Daniel Lance</a>. It launched in March 2024 with invitations to 100 retailers and first shipments that April. The cigars are made at <a href=\"/node/familiadisla/\">Tabacalera Familia Disla S.A.</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua.</p>\n<p>Domain debuted with two lines in the same nine sizes. <strong>Neutron</strong> is a medium-intensity blend: a Mexican San Andrés wrapper, a dual Nicaraguan binder, and fillers from Condega and Pueblo Nuevo. <strong>Negentropy</strong> uses an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper, a double Nicaraguan binder and Condega filler, and the company says it pairs \"impactful intensity\" with a clean finish. The sizes have science names, such as Correlation, Neural Plasticity and Simulation. A third blend, No7B, went out in the launch sampler as a possible future line. <strong>Entropy</strong>, built on U.S.-grown Connecticut broadleaf, followed later. The company describes its Despanic fermentation, which groups primings so oils and flavor pass between leaves.</p>\n<p>Disla brought decades of factory experience. He spent just over ten years at <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Fábrica de Tabacos Nica Sueño</a>, home of <a href=\"/node/romacraft/\">RoMa Craft Tobac</a>, and before that worked at Latin Cigars S.A., now <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>, blending for <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>, Toraño, <a href=\"/node/alecbradley/\">Alec Bradley</a> and some 30 other companies. Lance had 17 years in the industry, starting in retail at Outlaw Cigar Company and Fidel's Cigar Shop, and co-founded Lujo Concepts, maker of the BTX cutter.</p>",
  "elseptimo": "<p><strong>El Septimo</strong> is an ultra-premium cigar brand made in Costa Rica and owned since 2019 by <a href=\"/node/zayayounan/\">Zaya Younan</a>'s Younan Collection, through El Septimo Geneva SA. The French distributor Gilles Botquin founded it in 2005, and it was first produced at Pure Aroma Cigars S.A. in Costa Rica.</p>\n<p>In March 2019 the Younan Collection bought the brand's assets and renamed the business El Septimo Geneva SA. The company now says it rolls more than 2.5 million cigars a year at three Costa Rican factories, and that it handles farming, blending, aging and rolling in-house.</p>\n<p>The portfolio is built around themed collections with luxury packaging aimed at collectors. Gilgamesh, launched in 2020, was the company's first line in a 50 ring gauge. The company presented it as a response to U.S. demand for bigger cigars, since every earlier El Septimo had been thinner than 50. Sacred Arts followed in 2021 with seven cigars, and the Emperor Collection arrived in October 2022 with ten. Limited releases include Vartan the Dragon (2024, 999 boxes).</p>\n<p>Pure Aroma's owner, Isaias Santana Díaz of D'Crossier Cigars, is now an equal partner with <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> and Guy Fieri, the <a href=\"/node/knucklesandwich/\">Knuckle Sandwich</a> partners, in a factory in Santiago, Dominican Republic, that they planned to open in September 2026. Costa Rica's other collector-focused name on this map is <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a>, made by <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a> at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a> in Santiago de Puriscal.</p>",
  "laaurora": "<p><strong>La Aurora</strong> dates to 1903, when 18-year-old Eduardo León Jimenes opened a small factory in Guazumal, in the province of Santiago de los Caballeros. That makes it the oldest cigar company in the Dominican Republic. The factory soon moved into the city of Santiago. The first bands appeared in 1914, and the reclining lion, still the brand's symbol, first appeared on bands in 1926.</p>\n<p>La Aurora became the seed of the E. León Jimenes group, which grew into cigarettes, beer (it owned Presidente) and banking. Fernando León Asensio ran the tobacco side for almost half a century. His son <strong>Guillermo León</strong> took the lead in 1995, and in 2011 he bought 100% of La Aurora, separating premium cigars from the rest of the group. In 2007 the company had moved all its operations back to Guazumal, in the Tamboril free zone, where the <a href=\"/node/laaurorafactory/\">La Aurora Cigar Factory</a> is today.</p>\n<p>The lines follow that history. Preferidos, the double-figurado shape of the first 1903 cigars, is still rolled in small numbers. La Aurora 100 Años marked the 2003 centennial, and the 1495 Series honors Santiago's founding. The portfolio also includes León Jimenes (1987), the stronger 107 (2007), ADN Dominicano and Cameroon 1903. The factory rolls for outside brands such as <a href=\"/node/fratello/\">Fratello</a>, and La Aurora belongs to ProCigar alongside <a href=\"/node/lfd/\">La Flor Dominicana</a> and <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a>.</p>",
  "definition": "<p><strong>Definition Cigars</strong> is a Dallas-based boutique brand that <strong>Jamond Hackley</strong> launched in <strong>2019</strong>. Early releases included Conception (January 2020, named for the Ometepe region), The Equalizer (summer 2020), the 919 (blended for a September 19, 2020 celebration and released in 2021), Walking Stick (a lancero, January 2021), Pig, and Noir Cameroon. On shelves the brand is easy to spot: most releases wear fabric rope bands in bright colors instead of paper bands.</p>\n<p>The best-documented production tie is <a href=\"/node/lucianopichardo/\">Luciano Tabacos S.A.</a> in Estelí. Definition's GEN413 (first called Genesis) was made with Luciano Meirelles, Luciano's CEO and co-founder, and launched at the 2023 PCA show as a 5½ x 54 super robusto. It uses an Ecuadorian broadleaf hybrid wrapper, a Mexican San Andrés binder, and Nicaraguan fillers from Condega, Ometepe, Pueblo Nuevo, and Chincagre leaf from Masatepe. A 7 x 42 lancero and a 6 x 52 toro joined the line in 2024. A GEN413 Maduro, which swaps the wrapper and binder, followed in 2025.</p>\n<p>Newer lines come from a factory the company has not disclosed, and all use Nicaraguan filler. Anillo (2025, 6¼ x 54) is the brand's first cigar with a Pennsylvania broadleaf binder. Pi5ot and One13 (2026, both 6 x 52) use San Andrés wrappers. Pi5ot is a basketball tribute to Jalen Rose and Slippery Rock, and One13 is named for the address of Hackley's grandmother's house. On Cigar Nexus, Definition maps to Luciano for GEN413 only. Treat other lines as made in Nicaragua at an undisclosed factory.</p>",
  "lure": "<p><strong>Lure Cigars</strong> is a small Nicaraguan-made brand from Lou Cross III of Tallahassee, made since fall 2025 at <a href=\"/node/latabacaria/\">La Tabacaria</a> in Estelí and distributed by Valacari Cigars. Cross created it to honor his late father, Lou Cross Jr., a lifelong fisherman and cigar smoker who died in June 2023 shortly after turning 90.</p>\n<p>The brand pairs \"Tackle &amp; Tobacco\" with the tagline \"Smoke Great Cigars &amp; Tell Tall Tales.\" It soft-launched in September 2023 in ten-count bundles sealed in wax with Boveda packs.</p>\n<p>The first blend, Corojo Oscuro, is a Nicaraguan puro: a double-fermented Corojo Oscuro wrapper, Estelí binder, and Estelí, Jalapa and Ometepe filler, in fishing-camp sizes such as The Big Lou, The Lil' Tony and The Lunker. It was first made at <a href=\"/node/tabacalerapages/\">Tabacalera Pages de Nicaragua</a>, a factory Cross met through Valacari. A darker Oscuro line followed, along with the Connecticut broadleaf \"Last Cast,\" whose band carries 1933 and 2023 for Lou Jr.</p>\n<p>In fall 2025 Lure and Valacari moved production from Pages to La Tabacaria. The limited Catch &amp; Release series tests new sizes and blends. Its 2026 Slim Jim lonsdale (6½ x 42, Connecticut broadleaf) was capped at 1,800 cigars. Lure owns no factory, like most small brands on this map, such as <a href=\"/node/southerndraw/\">Southern Draw</a>.</p>",
  "gurkha": "<p><strong>Gurkha</strong> is one of the most widely distributed premium brands, known for ornate packaging and a very large catalog across price tiers. Its owner, Kaizad Hansotia, was selling watches and duty-free goods when, on a 1989 vacation in Goa, India, he bought a local vendor's cigars and the Gurkha name for about $149 (some accounts say $143, in 1988). He first gave the cigars to clients as gifts, then built a business around them, starting in duty-free shops in 1990.</p>\n<p>Hansotia made his name with extravagance. The Black Dragon Edition Speciale sold for $115,000 per humidor of 100 cigars, carved from camel bone, or about $1,150 a cigar. Regular lines such as Ghost (2012), Cellar Reserve and Royal Challenge carry more ordinary prices.</p>\n<p>Gurkha doesn't own a factory. It works as a negociant, matching lines to partner factories in the Dominican Republic, Nicaragua and Honduras. Production has included <a href=\"/node/williamventura/\">Tabacalera William Ventura</a> and <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Tabacalera La Alianza</a> (<a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a>'s factory, now Casa Carrillo) in the Dominican Republic, and <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a>, which made the Gurkha 125th. In Estelí, <a href=\"/node/caoamerican/\">American Caribbean</a> has made Cellar Reserve Platinum (with filler aged up to 12 years), Heritage Collection and Royal Challenge. More recently Colección Especial moved from PDR to <a href=\"/node/lapalma/\">Tabacalera Palma</a> in Tamboril.</p>",
  "lfd": "<p><strong>La Flor Dominicana (LFD)</strong> is made at the company's own factory, <a href=\"/node/lfdfactory/\">Tabacalera La Flor S.A.</a> in Tamboril, Dominican Republic. Much of the tobacco comes from its La Canela farm. LFD is the family company of <a href=\"/node/littogomez/\">Litto Gomez</a>, a former Miami jeweler, and his wife Inés Lorenzo-Gomez, who founded it in 1994. At first it was called Los Libertadores.</p>\n<p>LFD built its name on doing things Dominican cigars weren't known for. In 1997 Gomez bought La Canela, a farm near the factory, and began growing his own wrapper, binder and filler, which was rare for a Dominican boutique. The Double Ligero line pushed Dominican tobacco to full strength at a time when \"Dominican\" usually meant mild and Connecticut-wrapped. In 2003 LFD introduced the Chisel, a flattened, wedge-shaped head that Gomez patented and that became the brand's signature.</p>\n<p>The biggest win came in 2016, when the Andalusian Bull, a figurado, was named Cigar Aficionado's Cigar of the Year. The catalog also includes Ligero, Air Bender, Cameroon Cabinet, Coronado and Factory Press, much of it made from La Canela leaf.</p>\n<p>LFD remains family-run and owner-operated. That puts it in the same company as other Dominican family factories on the map, such as <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Arturo Fuente</a> and <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a>, rather than with the corporate groups.</p>",
  "ligaprivada": "<p><strong>Liga Privada</strong> is <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a>'s top traditional (uninfused) line, made at <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica Drew Estate</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua. It began in summer 2005 as the personal blend of <a href=\"/node/stevesaka/\">Steve Saka</a>, then Drew Estate's CEO.</p>\n<p>The No. 9 was the ninth recipe they tried. It debuted in 2006 and went on general sale in 2007, with a Connecticut broadleaf wrapper, a Brazilian Mata Fina binder and Honduran and Nicaraguan filler. The T52 followed in 2009, with a stalk-cut Connecticut Habano wrapper. H99 came in 2018. Único Serie is a separate set of limited shapes and blends sold under the Liga Privada name.</p>\n<p>Liga was scarce from the start, and that shaped the factory around it. By about 2009 demand was outrunning supply, and the rollers were asked to stop smoking Ligas on the floor. They made their own blend from other primings of the same tobaccos, and Drew Estate released it in 2011 as <a href=\"/node/undercrown/\">Undercrown</a>. <a href=\"/node/nickmelillo/\">Nick Melillo</a> worked on Liga production in his Drew Estate years.</p>\n<p>Liga Privada belongs to Drew Estate, which <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher International</a> has owned since 2014, and Saka left Drew Estate before founding Dunbarton Tobacco &amp; Trust. The cigar shows that the company known for infused <a href=\"/node/acid/\">Acid</a> also makes some of the most sought-after traditional cigars in Nicaragua.</p>",
  "caldwell": "<p>Caldwell Cigar Co. was started in 2013 by Robert Caldwell and a circle of friends who set out to do everything differently, a brainchild built on the founder's earlier ventures, including Wynwood Cigar Factory in Miami and The Hotel Humidor Company, a hospitality-centric cigar operation. Early industry confusion over the brand's packaging and purpose was, in Caldwell's telling, proof they were on the right track: unconventional presentation paired with proprietary, rarely available tobaccos rather than run-of-the-mill leaf everyone else uses.</p>\n<p>The company's stated mission is deliberately modest: a friends-run outfit that never chased scale, focused on making great cigars in exceptional packaging while doing good and having fun. That ethos extends beyond the humidor, Caldwell has backed art initiatives and charitable work through Lost &amp; Found cigars, with ongoing efforts around beach cleanups, homeless hunger outreach, and inner-city education. Core lines such as Blind Man's Bluff, Long Live the King, Lost &amp; Found, and Eastern Standard deliver the \"new experience\" the brand promised at launch, blends as distinctive as the boxes they ship in.</p>\n<p>On the manufacturing side, Caldwell operates as a modern negociant: matching each project to the right rolling floor rather than owning a single factory. Production flows through <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a> in the Dominican Republic, <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Fábrica de Tabacos Nica Sueño</a> in Estelí, <a href=\"/node/williamventura/\">Tabacalera William Ventura</a> in Santiago, and collaboration work at <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Tabacalera La Alianza (Casa Carrillo)</a>. That multi-factory pattern on the map is the point, serious boutique brands often span countries and partners behind one cohesive marque.</p>",
  "crownedheads": "<p><strong>Crowned Heads</strong> is a Nashville boutique cigar company that owns no factory. Its cigars are made by Ernesto Perez-Carrillo at <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a> (then Tabacalera La Alianza) in Santiago, Dominican Republic, and by the García family at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua, with some lines at other Estelí factories.</p>\n<p>The company came out of <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>. Late in 2010, as CAO was caught in the Swedish Match and STG merger and moved from Nashville to Richmond, Virginia, CAO's Jon Huber and Mike Conder decided to go independent. Conder had been CAO's senior vice president of marketing, and creative staffers Michael Trebing and Nancy Heathman came with them. Crowned Heads launched in 2011.</p>\n<p>Its first cigar, Four Kicks, is named for a Kings of Leon song. It was blended by Perez-Carrillo and was the first cigar his new factory made for another company: an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler, bunched entubado. Headley Grange and J.D. Howard Reserve followed from the same factory, which made all of Crowned Heads' cigars until 2014. That year Las Calaveras, a limited edition, and Jericho Hill, named from Johnny Cash's \"Cocaine Blues,\" became its first cigars from My Father.</p>\n<p>Later partners include <a href=\"/node/lucianopichardo/\">Tabacalera Pichardo</a>, which made Mil Dias, and <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a>, where Le Pâtissier moved. Crowned Heads also distributed Tim Ozgener's <a href=\"/node/ozgener/\">Ozgener Family Cigars</a> Bosphorus at launch. An earlier version of this page said Jas Sum Kral is made at My Father and that Huber came from Drew Estate. Neither is right, and both have been removed.</p>",
  "southerndraw": "<p><strong>Southern Draw Cigars</strong> are made by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua, which the company calls its exclusive factory partner. Southern Draw is a U.S. veteran-owned company founded in 2014 by husband and wife Robert and Sharon Holt and run with family and close friends.</p>\n<p>The core portfolio follows a botanical theme: Kudzu, Firethorn, Rose of Sharon, Jacob's Ladder, Cedrus and the value-minded Quickdraw. Rose of Sharon is named for Sharon Holt, and Jacob's Ladder, wrapped in Pennsylvania broadleaf, nods to their son Ethan \"Jacob\" Holt. The first Kudzu was made at Fernández's factory in 2014, and Rose of Sharon and Jacob's Ladder followed in 2016. Cigar Aficionado has scored Jacob's Ladder Brimstone 93, Kudzu 92 and Cedrus 91.</p>\n<p>That puts Southern Draw among the outside brands A.J. Fernández rolls, alongside names such as <a href=\"/node/guayacan/\">Guayacan</a> and <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas</a>, rather than a factory owner. Fernández has a second factory, <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> in Ocotal, where he makes contract brands such as <a href=\"/node/knucklesandwich/\">Knuckle Sandwich</a>; Southern Draw's regular lines come from Estelí.</p>\n<p>The tenth-anniversary Decennium (2024) was the exception: a 6 x 46 corona extra made at <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a> in Miami's Little Havana, with a Brazilian Arapiraca wrapper, Dominican Criollo 98 binder and Nicaraguan filler.</p>",
  "kristoff": "<p><strong>Kristoff Cigars</strong> was founded in 2004 by Glen Case, who spent about 19 years in corporate finance, much of it in banking in Chicago, before he became an independent cigar sales rep. Six months in, he decided to launch his own line. The brand is named after his son, Christopher, and the Kristania line is inspired by his daughter, Brittany. Kristoff is run from an office in Warrenville, Illinois, outside Chicago.</p>\n<p>Case had hoped to buy a factory in the Dominican Republic. Instead he partnered with Rolando Villamil, who ran the Charles Fairmorn factory in Santiago, now <a href=\"/node/voneicken/\">Tabacalera Von Eicken</a>. It is the only factory that has ever made Kristoff. Villamil oversaw sourcing, blending and quality control there until his death in February 2022, and Case calls him his mentor. Kristoff started with mixed-filler Dominican bundles. By 2018 Case was selling about 2 million cigars a year, and by 2024 the brand was sold in 50 to 60 countries.</p>\n<p>An earlier version of this page credited Kristoff to Lissette Perez-Carrillo, a daughter of <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a>. It also linked the brand to <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a> and to <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR Cigars</a>. Every source we found names Glen Case as founder and the Fairmorn factory as Kristoff's only maker, so those links have been removed. Villamil also oversaw Epic Cigars at the same factory.</p>",
  "montecristonc": "<p><strong>Montecristo (Non-Cuban)</strong> is the U.S. and non-embargo expression of one of the world's most famous cigar names, entirely separate from Habanos S.A.'s Cuban Montecristo. In the United States the marca is controlled by <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> (Imperial Brands / Tabacalera USA), <em>not</em> by <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a> or <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>. That ownership fact is frequently confused online; STG holds other legacy names (Cohiba NC, Partagas NC, Punch NC), while Montecristo sits on the Imperial/Altadis side of the split with Romeo y Julieta and Trinidad.</p>\n<p>Non-Cuban Montecristo spans approachable Dominican core lines and ambitious Nicaraguan projects. Landmark collaborations include work with the <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a> family (Espada and related Nicaraguan puros) and high-scoring releases blended with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> in Estelí, which makes the Nicaragua Series (2018). The Montecristo 1935 Anniversary Nicaragua No. 2 was made at his <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal. Product chief Rafael Nodal and Fernandez also partnered on related Altadis programs (Romeo San Andrés, later Trinidad Espiritu), making Montecristo part of a deliberate strategy: heritage trademark, Estelí fire, and modern packaging for U.S. retail.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Montecristo (NC) is an ownership and production story. Altadis trademark control, multi-country manufacture (Dominican volume plus Nicaraguan prestige projects), and a hard line between this brand and the Cuban factory system that still defines the name outside the U.S. Use this node with <a href=\"/node/ryjnc/\">Romeo y Julieta (NC)</a> and <a href=\"/node/trinidadespiritu/\">Trinidad Espiritu</a> for the Altadis cluster, and with STG's Cohiba/Partagas pages when mapping the full Cuban-legacy trademark landscape.</p><p>Montecristo is among the most searched cigar names on earth, which makes ownership accuracy non-negotiable. A U.S. Montecristo is an Altadis/Imperial product, not an STG cigar and not a Habanos export. Cuban Montecristo never appears legally in the United States; the non-Cuban line fills that demand with Dominican volume SKUs and Nicaraguan prestige projects that have earned serious critic scores. Treat Espada, 1935 Anniversary, and core White/Classic lines as one trademark family with very different factory stories underneath.</p>",
  "ryjnc": "<p><strong>Romeo y Julieta (Non-Cuban)</strong> carries the 1875-era name into the modern U.S. market under <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> (Imperial Brands / Tabacalera USA). It is parallel to, but legally and operationally separate from, Habanos S.A.'s Cuban Romeo y Julieta. Familiar U.S. lines such as <strong>1875</strong>, <strong>Reserva Real</strong>, Vintage, and related extensions emphasize approachable to medium-plus profiles for broad retail distribution: the everyday counterpart to Cuban Churchill mythology rather than a boutique Estelí cult brand.</p>\n<p>While much historical volume has been Dominican, Altadis has repeatedly tapped Nicaraguan capacity for prestige projects. This map records contract production ties to <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a>, whose multi-country factories support many outside brands. High-profile work with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> (including Romeo San Andrés) sits in the same Altadis strategy that produced Montecristo Nicaragua programs and <a href=\"/node/trinidadespiritu/\">Trinidad Espiritu</a> at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> in Estelí: corporate trademark owner, product leadership from Rafael Nodal, and Estelí blending talent for lines that need more power than classic Dominican mildness.</p>\n<p>For ownership mapping, Romeo y Julieta (NC) lives on the <strong>Altadis / Imperial</strong> side of the Cuban-legacy split, opposite STG/General/Forged portfolios for Cohiba, Partagas, and Punch. That is the graph's core teaching: same historic names, two conglomerates, two manufacturing webs, zero connection to Habanos production for U.S. legal product.</p><p>Romeo y Julieta 1875 remains a default recommendation in countless U.S. shops, so the brand carries continuous demand from new smokers and gift buyers. Stronger limiteds and San Andrés-era projects show Altadis using Nicaraguan partners when the catalog needs more than mild Dominican comfort. Cross-read this node with Montecristo (NC) and Trinidad Espiritu to see how one Imperial product organization manages multiple Cuban-legacy names without mixing their factories or trademark paths with STG's portfolio.</p>",
  "fratello": "<p><strong>Fratello Cigars</strong> was founded in 2013 by Omar de Frias. He was born in Puerto Rico, grew up in the Dominican Republic and played professional basketball there, then earned an MBA at the University of Puerto Rico and joined NASA in 2004. He says he managed a five-billion-dollar budget there. He started Fratello while still at NASA, working four 12-hour days to free up Fridays for selling cigars, and left NASA in 2016 to run the brand full time.</p>\n<p>Veteran salesman José Blanco introduced him to <a href=\"/node/alejandromartinez/\">Alejandro Martínez Cuenca</a> and Juan Ignacio Martínez of <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a>. Joya made the first cigar, Fratello Classico, a medium-bodied blend of Nicaraguan, Ecuadorian and Peruvian tobacco in bright red boxes. In 2019 most Fratello cigars were still made at Joya, including Navetta, Bianco and Arlequín.</p>\n<p>Some lines come from the Dominican Republic. Fratello Oro and the Texas-exclusive The Texan (2019) are made at <a href=\"/node/laaurorafactory/\">La Aurora</a>, as are the Camo bundle blends. In 2023 Fratello added Camo Nicaragua sizes made at Karen Berger's Estelí Cigars. A friend from de Frias's NASA days put the Fratello logo on the Odysseus lander, which touched down on the moon on February 22, 2024. Earlier versions of this map linked Fratello to <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a>; we found no source for that, so the link has been removed.</p>",
  "padilla": "<p><strong>Padilla Cigars</strong> was founded in 2003 by Miami's Ernesto Padilla, son of the Cuban poet Heberto Padilla, with his brother Carlos. Ernesto created the brand to honor his late father, whose persecution under Castro became known as the Padilla Affair. Padilla has always been a contract brand, and its list of factories traces several eras of boutique cigar-making.</p>\n<p>The early hits, including Padilla Miami, 1932 and 1948, were made with <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a>, who rolled for Padilla from 2005 until 2008. Cigar Aficionado listed Padilla among the brands García rolled under contract at his Calle Ocho factory, <a href=\"/node/elreyhabanos/\">El Rey de los Habanos</a>, alongside <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a>. When García stopped in 2008, citing demand from his other brands, Padilla opened Fábrica de Tabacos Padilla on Calle Ocho, across from Domino Park, to make Miami, 1932 and 1948. Series '68 kept coming from Tabacalera Aguilar, a small father-and-son factory in Honduras, though Padilla said it used no Honduran tobacco. Padilla Habano was rolled in Nicaragua at Oliva.</p>\n<p>By 2011 the flagship lines were rolled at <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> in Danlí with Aganorsa tobacco, and other lines at Tabacalera Tropical, Aganorsa's Miami factory. Aganorsa owner Eduardo Fernández has said that for a time he had cigars made at Raíces with his own tobacco and blends. In 2012 Padilla discontinued those lines and moved again. Miami 8&amp;11 Maduro and Invictus went to <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a>, the last of the old Calle Ocho factories, and Padilla Reserva went to Oliva's <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> in Estelí. Few boutique brands have worked with as many of the industry's best-known factories.</p>",
  "ozgener": "<p><strong>Ozgener Family Cigars</strong> is the brand Tim Ozgener launched in 2022, his first blend in roughly 12 years. The name carries history: his father, Cano Ozgener, founded CAO International in Nashville in 1968 as a pipe business, and the family launched <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a> cigars in 1995 before selling the company to Henri Wintermans in 2007. Tim left after CAO moved to Richmond, Virginia, in 2010, following the Swedish Match and Scandinavian Tobacco merger that created today's <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>.</p>\n<p>The debut line, <strong>Bosphorus</strong>, was first rolled at <a href=\"/node/lucianopichardo/\">Tabacalera Pichardo</a> in Estelí. In late 2022 production moved to Tabacalera La Alianza, the Dominican factory of <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a> (<a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo / La Alianza</a>), and the wrapper changed from Ecuador Sumatra to Ecuador Habano 2000 while the filler stayed Nicaraguan. <strong>Aramas</strong> (2023) is also made at La Alianza, with a Mexican San Andrés wrapper, Ecuador Sumatra binder, and Dominican, Nicaraguan, and Connecticut broadleaf filler. The name joins Ararat and Masis, the Turkish and Armenian names for the same mountain, a nod to the family's Armenian and Turkish roots.</p>\n<p>Tim Ozgener is also a partner in Nashville's <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>, which shared trade-show booths with Ozgener and distributed Bosphorus at launch.</p>",
  "jassumkral": "<p><strong>Jas Sum Kral</strong> cigars are made in Estelí, Nicaragua, at a factory the brand has not named. It is the boutique brand of Riste Ristevski, who grew up in Macedonia and worked in IT in the United States before going into cigars. He founded it in 2015. The name means \"I Am King\" in Macedonian, and the bands are by the Singapore designer Nuzli Hakiim.</p>\n<p>Lines include Red Knight (Ecuadorian Habano wrapper), Zlatno Sonce, Crna Nok, Kralot (San Andrés wrapper) and Tyrannical Buc (Connecticut broadleaf maduro).</p>\n<p>The brand has changed factories twice. From 2015 to 2017 the cigars were made at <a href=\"/node/neworder/\">NOA</a>, the Estelí factory of <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a>. On January 1, 2018 production moved to <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragon</a>, also in Estelí. On October 11, 2024 Jas Sum Kral left Aragon after what it called a six-month effort to reblend every line. \"I am no longer associated with Tabacalera Aragon S.A.,\" Ristevski said. The company's site now says only that the cigars come from \"a factory in Estelí,\" so Cigar Nexus shows no current factory.</p>\n<p>The reblended cigars returned in 2025, with shipments to the Netherlands that spring. For its tenth year the brand, long sold direct to customers, partnered with CigarPage for U.S. sales. Earlier versions of this page linked Jas Sum Kral to <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a>. No source we found supports that, so those links are gone.</p>",
  "stolenthrone": "<p><strong>Stolen Throne Cigars</strong> was founded by Lee Marsh. Before making cigars, Marsh worked in compliance and tech, and he spent much of his travel time in cigar lounges around the world. He has said that a visit to a tobacco farm in the Dominican Republic started him thinking about making his own cigars. Through mutual friends, including <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island\" Jim Robinson</a>, he met <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a>. Marsh says he was in Nicaragua a week later, starting work on the first cigar.</p>\n<p>That cigar, Crook of the Crown, is still the flagship. It has a Mexican San Andrés maduro wrapper, an Indonesian binder and Nicaraguan filler, and it is rolled at the <a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas Cigar Factory</a> in Estelí. In 2024 the company marked its fifth anniversary with the Crook of the Crown Corona, limited to 50,000 cigars. That puts the brand's start around 2019, not 2018 as this page used to say.</p>\n<p>Other lines include Castle 919, 3 Kingdoms, Call to Arms, Yorktown Fleet (2023) and the limited Phantom Queen. Marsh describes the name as being about making your own place at the table, in an outlaw, Waylon Jennings style. Stolen Throne is one of several boutique brands built with Rojas, whose older factory, <a href=\"/node/neworder/\">NOA</a>, also made the first <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a> cigars.</p>",
  "deadwood": "<p><strong>Deadwood Tobacco Co.</strong> is a <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> brand made at <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica Drew Estate</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua. It is named for Deadwood, South Dakota, the Black Hills town known as a biker destination.</p>\n<p>Its first three cigars, Sweet Jane, Fat Bottom Betty and Crazy Alice, were marketed together as the \"Yummy Bitches.\" Each is a maduro-wrapped cigar blended with aromatic tobaccos that bring out natural sweetness, and each has its own \"vixen\" character on the band and box.</p>\n<p>Sweet Jane is a mellow 5 x 46 corona. Fat Bottom Betty, a medium-to-full robusto (5 x 54, with toro and gordito sizes), uses a Connecticut broadleaf maduro wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler, with notes of wood, leather, pepper and coffee. Crazy Alice is a 4½ x 46 with chocolate and dried-fruit notes. Leather Rose, a full-bodied 5 x 54 torpedo, joined the family in 2020, and Baby Jane is a 4 x 32 cigarillo sold in tins.</p>\n<p>Deadwood sits between Drew Estate's two traditions. It isn't infused like <a href=\"/node/acid/\">Acid</a>, but it leans sweet in a way <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a> and <a href=\"/node/undercrown/\">Undercrown</a> don't, and all of them come from the same factory. La Gran Fábrica opened in July 2007, and Drew Estate has been owned by <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher International</a> since 2014.</p>",
  "leafbyoscar": "<p><strong>Leaf by Oscar</strong> is the brand <a href=\"/node/oscarvalladares/\">Oscar Valladares</a> created with Pittsburgh retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island Jim\" Robinson</a>, made at Valladares's factory in Danlí, Honduras. Its story starts with Valladares's first cigar, 2012. Each 2012 box held one standout cigar wrapped in a whole tobacco leaf instead of cellophane, a Mayan-themed \"last smoke\" for the predicted end of the world on December 21, 2012.</p>\n<p>Robinson brought a group to Honduras, liked the cigar and bought 30 boxes. He then asked for a house blend for his Leaf &amp; Bean shops, called Leaf &amp; Bean by Oscar, and ordered 5,000 cigars. He took 1,000 home and sold out within a week. Customers loved the leaf wrapping, which Valladares and his master blender Bayron Duarte had dreamed up for the 2012 box. When other shops wanted the cigar, Valladares dropped the shop name and called it Leaf by Oscar. Robinson opened about 100 accounts in three months, and by 2017 the cigars were in about 1,000 U.S. shops.</p>\n<p>The line comes in Connecticut, Maduro, Corojo and Sumatra wrappers over Honduran tobacco. Its growth helped Valladares move into the old Oliva factory in Danlí in January 2015. A 10th Anniversary Criollo followed at PCA 2023. Robinson's sister brand is <a href=\"/node/islandjim/\">Island Jim</a>, which started as the Island Jim #2 private label.</p>",
  "oscarvalladares": "<p><strong>Oscar Valladares Tobacco &amp; Co.</strong> makes its cigars at its own factory in Danlí, Honduras, now in a building that Oliva once used. It is the company of Oscar Valladares, who makes his own lines there, such as 2012, The Oscar and Super Fly, plus <a href=\"/node/leafbyoscar/\">Leaf by Oscar</a> and <a href=\"/node/islandjim/\">Island Jim</a>.</p>\n<p>Valladares started out driving visitors around Honduras for <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel</a>. Patel later gave him a job in quality control, and Valladares worked with the company until 2011. He started his own production in Danlí in 2012 with the 2012 by Oscar, a cigar wrapped in an unfermented leaf instead of cellophane, timed to the Mayan-calendar date of December 21, 2012.</p>\n<p>The 2012 led to his best-known partnership. Pittsburgh retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island Jim\" Robinson</a> brought a group to Honduras, bought 30 boxes of the 2012 for his Leaf &amp; Bean shop, and then ordered a leaf-wrapped private blend. The first 1,000 cigars sold out in about a week and a half in 2013. When other shops wanted it, the cigar was renamed Leaf by Oscar.</p>\n<p>House lines include The Oscar (2016), Super Fly, Rosalila (2013) and Altar Q. The last two are named for monuments at the Mayan site of Copán, where the company grows about 200 acres of tobacco, currently used as filler, and ferments leaf.</p>",
  "islandjim": "<p><strong>Island Jim</strong> cigars are made by <a href=\"/node/oscarvalladares/\">Oscar Valladares</a> at his factory in Danlí, Honduras. The brand is named for <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island Jim\" Robinson</a>, the Pittsburgh retailer behind the Leaf &amp; Bean shops</p>\n<p>It grew out of the same partnership as <a href=\"/node/leafbyoscar/\">Leaf by Oscar</a>. Robinson brought a group to Honduras, liked Valladares's first cigar, 2012, and bought 30 boxes. He then ordered a house blend wrapped in a whole tobacco leaf, Leaf &amp; Bean by Oscar. When other shops wanted it, Valladares renamed it Leaf by Oscar. Oscar Valladares Tobacco &amp; Co. later moved into a former Oliva factory in Danlí. Island Jim #2 is named for its shape, a torpedo meant to evoke a No. 2 pencil.</p>\n<p>The line now includes Island Jim No. 2 and Connecticut #Two, a 6½ x 52 torpedo with a Honduran Connecticut wrapper that debuted at the 2023 Tobacco Plus Expo for about $12 in 21-count boxes. The 2025 Island Jim Barber Pole (6 x 52) twists Honduran Connecticut and Corojo wrappers together over an undisclosed blend. It debuted at the 2025 PCA show alongside the Leaf by Oscar Broadleaf Limited Edition. A newer Leaf &amp; Bean by Island Jim shop, founded in 2024 in St. Augustine, Florida, carries the line.</p>",
  "lhpremium": "<p><strong>LH Premium Cigars</strong> are made in Costa Rica at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a> in Puriscal. The brand belongs to <a href=\"/node/nicholassyris/\">Nicholas Syris</a>, a partner in the Lavida Habana cigar lounges in several Middle Eastern countries; \"LH\" stands for Lavida Habana.</p>\n<p>The lounges sold custom-blended Cuban house cigars. Syris wanted to bring that idea to the U.S. market with non-Cuban tobacco. The Claro and Maduro lines reached the U.S. in summer 2014, and by July 2015 they were in about 60 stores. LH made its IPCPR trade show debut that year with a new Colorado blend: an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper over a Nicaraguan binder, with filler from Peru, Ecuador and Nicaragua.</p>\n<p>In 2016 the three blends shared seven sizes after a lancero and three more sizes were added, along with new boxes and bands. The Claro uses Ecuadorian Connecticut shade and the Maduro an Ecuadorian Criollo maduro wrapper. LH Limited Edition followed in the U.S. in 2020.</p>\n<p>Tabacos de Costa Rica, the Olman family's factory, also makes <a href=\"/node/casdagli/\">Casdagli</a>'s Villa Casdagli, <a href=\"/node/lampert/\">Lampert</a> and <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>'s brands. Syris also made <a href=\"/node/nickandjim/\">Nick &amp; Jim</a> there with Pittsburgh retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island\" Jim Robinson</a>. It was announced in 2017 as Lavida Isla and renamed before it shipped.</p>",
  "nickandjim": "<p><strong>Nick &amp; Jim</strong> is a collaboration between <a href=\"/node/nicholassyris/\">Nicholas Syris</a> of <a href=\"/node/lhpremium/\">LH Premium Cigars</a> and <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island\" Jim Robinson</a> of Leaf &amp; Bean, a cigar shop in Pittsburgh. It was announced in July 2017 as Lavida Isla, a combination of Syris's Lavida Habana and Robinson's island-life persona. Naming-rights problems meant that by December, when it shipped, it was called Nick &amp; Jim's P.B.E., for \"pre-band edition.\"</p>\n<p>\"We decided to take a challenge put in front of us and turn it into a positive,\" Syris said. The cigar was the same blend that was meant to be Lavida Isla: an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper and Ecuadorian binder over filler from Nicaragua and Peru. It launched as a single 6 x 54 toro at $9.95, in boxes of 20 with a deliberately plain, rustic band. Robinson's distribution company, 3R, handled sales. A torpedo and a 6 x 60 size were announced in 2018.</p>\n<p>The cigars are rolled in Costa Rica at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, the factory that also makes the LH lines. Nick &amp; Jim is its own node on this map. It is Costa Rican production, separate from Robinson's other projects, which are linked to factories in Honduras.</p>",
  "lampert": "<p><strong>Lampert Cigars</strong> was founded in 2017 by Dr. Stefan Lampert, a Liechtenstein-born attorney. The company says the idea came on a 2016 trip to Thailand, where bespoke tailors made him wonder whether cigars could be made like custom suits. The first result was a line called My Cigar Lab, later renamed the <strong>Oro Line</strong>. The company is based in Panama City, Panama.</p>\n<p>Lampert's Costa Rican cigars are rolled at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, the same factory that makes <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a> and <a href=\"/node/byron/\">Byron</a> for <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>, as well as <a href=\"/node/vegassantiago/\">Vegas de Santiago</a>. The Oro Line uses an Ecuador Connecticut wrapper, an Ecuador Habano 2000 binder and Dominican and Peruvian fillers aged four to five years, in Señorito, El Gringo and Don Patrón sizes.</p>\n<p><strong>Ocean Breeze</strong> came from a sample blend with a salty, ocean-like note. The updated 5 x 54 Robusto Grande shipped in November 2022, and in August 2025 Lampert added the Gordito (4 x 58) and Toro Grande (6½ x 56), shown first at InterTabac in Dortmund.</p>\n<p>Not everything is Costa Rican. Lampert says its 1675 line (Edición Azul, Rojo, Morado and Verde) is made with the Ortez family in Nicaragua, and the portfolio also includes Dominican cigars, so the brand now produces in three countries.</p>",
  "casdagli": "<p><strong>Casdagli Cigars</strong> was founded by Jeremy Casdagli in 1997. His family was based in Cairo and Manchester in the 1800s, trading grain and cotton and breeding Arabian horses, and it later added Turkish pipe tobacco. The family left Egypt after the Suez Crisis, and its house in Cairo, Villa Casdagli, is still a city landmark. Emmanuel Theodore Casdagli, working with the British Board of Trade, negotiated with Cuba's pre-revolutionary government in 1951 to end Britain's wartime embargo on Cuban cigars.</p>\n<p>In 1997 in Havana, Jeremy met the Cuban master roller Carlos Valdez Mosquera, who rolled a small private supply of 500 to 1,000 cigars a month for London clients. When Mosquera retired in 2013, Casdagli moved to Hendrik Kelner Jr.'s Kelner Boutique Factory in the Dominican Republic. There they created the Traditional (2013), Club Mareva (2014), Basilica (2015) and Cabinet Selection (2016) lines, with Grand Café as the signature cigar.</p>\n<p>Daughters of the Wind (2018) is made at IGM Cigars in Costa Rica. Villa Casdagli debuted at the 2021 TPE show and is made at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, which Casdagli chose for its capacity and fermentation. Many of its blends lean on Peruvian tobacco and on traditional sizes such as the lancero. Tabacos de Costa Rica also makes <a href=\"/node/lhpremium/\">LH Premium</a> and <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>'s brands.</p>",
  "vegassantiago": "<p><strong>Vegas de Santiago</strong> is a Costa Rican cigar brand from Santiago de Puriscal, about an hour from San José, where tobacco grows on volcanic mountain slopes at roughly 1,100 meters. Lines include El Magnifico \"Heritage\" (Costa Rican filler and binder under an Ecuadorian maduro wrapper), El Unico \"D8\" (Ecuadorian Habano 2000 wrapper), Don Luis Gold Label, El Mystico, El Puro and El Primero. The brand began as a growers' cooperative in Puriscal. Don Luis Santana Lamas, a former H. Upmann production manager in Cuba, joined it and taught its members how to grow, cure and roll cigar tobacco.</p>\n<p>According to its German importer, the cigars are rolled in Puriscal by <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, a family business dating to 1997. The brand was co-founded by Rudy Niehaus and the founder of Tabacos de Costa Rica, and Rudy's son Marc Niehaus now commissions production there. The brand's own history says Rudolf Niehaus secured its international sales rights and helped save the factory during a loan crisis, and that Marc later became a partner and international sales manager. A German retailer who met the factory's Señor Olman in Puriscal about 20 years ago remembers him as head of Vegas de Santiago at the time. Today the Olman family owns the factory, and Vegas de Santiago is a client.</p>\n<p>For years Vegas de Santiago was the Costa Rican brand most German smokers knew. It shares a roof with far pricier cigars: the same factory makes <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a> and <a href=\"/node/byron/\">Byron</a> for <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>, along with boutiques such as <a href=\"/node/lampert/\">Lampert</a>. Vegas de Santiago keeps to the local end of that range: Costa Rican leaf, Puriscal rolling and everyday prices.</p>",
  "atabey": "<p><strong>Atabey</strong> is the flagship of <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>, the Costa Rican company owned by master blender <a href=\"/node/nelsonalfonso/\">Nelson Alfonso</a>. Alfonso is known for designing the Cohiba Behike brand. Atabey is named for the Taíno mother goddess. The cigars are made at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a> in Santiago de Puriscal and distributed in the U.S. by United Cigars of New Hampshire.</p>\n<p>Aging is the brand's signature. Atabey launched in 2010 with three sizes that had been rolled in 2005 and then aged for five years. Alfonso says the company normally ages its cigars five years in a cedar-lined room after rolling. The blend uses an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper with some Peruvian ligero in the filler; the rest is undisclosed. The range now runs to 12 vitolas with ritual names such as Ritos, Delirios, Brujos and Dioses. Dioses, an 8 x 50 double corona, was rolled in 2013 and released in 2018. In 2023 a Dioses 10-Year Extra Aged from that first 2013 batch came out at $36 a cigar, limited to 370 boxes of 25.</p>\n<p>The same factory rolls Selected Tobacco's <a href=\"/node/byron/\">Byron</a> as well as outside brands such as <a href=\"/node/lampert/\">Lampert</a> and <a href=\"/node/vegassantiago/\">Vegas de Santiago</a>. That makes Puriscal one of the few Costa Rican addresses known for high-end cigars. Atabey sits at the top of that roster, with prices and box counts closer to collector releases than everyday smokes.</p>",
  "latelier": "<p><strong>L'Atelier</strong> (French for \"the workshop\") is <a href=\"/node/petejohnson/\">Pete Johnson</a>'s second company, launched in 2012 as L'Atelier Imports. It is a sister company to <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a>, not a Tatuaje line extension. Johnson started it with his brother K.C. and friends including Dan Welsh (Surrogates). The flagship line debuted at the 2012 IPCPR show in three sizes, LAT52, LAT54 and LAT56. The sizes were a wink at Cohiba Behike vitolas, pigtail caps and all, but the blend is Nicaraguan, not Cuban.</p>\n<p>The LAT56 reached No. 12 on Cigar Aficionado's Top 25 for 2013. The LAT46 Selection Spéciale added a smaller size wrapped in a higher priming of the same leaf. La Mission followed in 2015, and later lines include Côte d'Or, a Maduro and the Surrogates series.</p>\n<p>The L'Atelier signature, except on Surrogates, is Sancti Spíritus, a finicky Criollo and Pelo de Oro hybrid grown in Ecuador and used as wrapper or in the blend. That leaf and the wine-country names are meant to set the workshop apart from tattoo-themed Tatuaje, while the construction is the same.</p>\n<p>Almost everything is rolled at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí by the <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">García</a> family, the same factory that made Tatuaje famous. L'Atelier is Johnson's own company, not an Altadis or STG brand. It is a García-made sibling of Ashton's <a href=\"/node/sancristobal/\">San Cristóbal</a> and of Tatuaje.</p>",
  "hoyonc": "<p><strong>Hoyo de Monterrey (non-Cuban)</strong> is made in Danlí, Honduras, at the <a href=\"/node/stgdanli/\">STG Danlí (HATSA)</a> factory, and owned by <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>, part of <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. It is the U.S. version of the brand, not the Cuban Hoyo made by Habanos S.A.</p>\n<p>The brand came to Honduras because of the Cuban embargo. Tampa's Villazon &amp; Co., run by Frank Llaneza and Dan Blumenthal, bought the U.S. rights to Hoyo de Monterrey and Punch from Fernando Palicio about 1965. Palicio had owned the Cuban brands before the revolution. Llaneza had already started Honduras American Tabaco S.A. (HATSA) in Danlí around 1964, so the cigars were rolled there from the start. By the late 1990s Villazon was making about 125,000 handmade cigars a day in Honduras, mostly Punch and Hoyo.</p>\n<p>General Cigar agreed in 1996 to buy Villazon for $91 million, and the deal closed in January 1997. It included the Danlí factory and the U.S. rights to Hoyo, Punch and El Rey del Mundo. Recent Hoyo releases, including Epicure Selección and the 2024 Rojo and Oscuro, are still rolled at the Danlí factory. Older lines include Excalibur and Hoyo de Tradición. Hoyo La Amistad was a collaboration with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> in Nicaragua. <a href=\"/node/punch_nc/\">Punch</a> remains Hoyo's sibling from the Villazon days.</p>",
  "hupmannnc": "<p><strong>H. Upmann (non-Cuban)</strong> is made for <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> in two places: at <a href=\"/node/tabdegarcia/\">Tabacalera de García</a> in La Romana, Dominican Republic, and, for its Nicaraguan lines, by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> in Estelí. It shares its name, but not its cigars, with the Cuban H. Upmann that Habanos S.A. sells.</p>\n<p>The name goes back to Hermann Upmann, a German banker who in 1844 invested in a Havana cigar factory. The Dominican H. Upmann line includes Vintage Cameroon, 1844 Reserve and The Banker, a regular line launched in 2014 as a tribute to the founder. Its limited Ingot editions followed, including the pink-boxed Banker Ingot Rosé of 2017, made at Tabacalera de García with a Cameroon wrapper.</p>\n<p>The Nicaraguan chapter began in 2017. H. Upmann by AJ Fernandez uses an Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper, a Nicaraguan Corojo '99 binder and filler from Nicaragua and the Dominican Republic. It shipped in May 2017 in Churchill, Toro and Robusto sizes for $7.25 to $7.75, as a national Altadis release rather than a catalog exclusive. Later Nicaraguan releases include Heritage (2022). <a href=\"/node/rafaelnodal/\">Rafael Nodal</a>, Tabacalera USA's head of product capability, is the public face of those Fernández projects, which also include <a href=\"/node/montecristonc/\">Montecristo Nicaragua</a> and <a href=\"/node/trinidadespiritu/\">Trinidad Espiritu</a>.</p>",
  "agingroom": "<p><strong>Aging Room</strong> is the boutique marca created by <a href=\"/node/rafaelnodal/\">Rafael Nodal</a> and launched in <strong>2011</strong> under his Miami company <a href=\"/node/boutiqueblends/\">Boutique Blends</a> (the former Habana Cuba Cigar Co.). The idea was small-batch, extra-aged tobacco rather than a giant catalog: rare lots, limited runs, and blends named like a music program. It worked. Cigar Aficionado placed early Aging Room cigars in its Top 25, named the Quattro F55 Concerto No. 2 of 2013, and later named the Quattro Nicaragua Maestro No. 1 Cigar of the Year in 2019 (96 points). That last cigar is the one most smokers mean when they say Aging Room today.</p>\n<p>Ownership and distribution are easy to mix up. Nodal still owns Aging Room. <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> has distributed the brand since <strong>2017</strong>, and Nodal is also Altadis vice president of product capability, blending heritage projects such as Montecristo Nicaragua and Trinidad Espiritu. The company is not an Altadis house marca the way Montecristo (NC) is. Production splits by line. Core Dominican Aging Room (Quattro Original, Small Batch M356, Solera, and related) is rolled with José \"Jochy\" Blanco at <a href=\"/node/lapalma/\">Tabacalera Palma</a> in Tamboril. The Nicaraguan flagship, Quattro Nicaragua, and the 2025 La Boheme reboot are made with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a> at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> in Estelí. Cigar Aficionado has also noted some Aging Room work with Nestor Plasencia Jr., so the graph includes a lighter <a href=\"/node/plasenciaesteli/\">Plasencia Estelí</a> production edge.</p>\n<p>On Cigar Nexus, Aging Room is the boutique node that sits on both the Dominican Palma floor and the Estelí A.J. contract web, owned by Nodal, sold through Altadis. Map it when asking who makes Quattro Nicaragua, whether Aging Room is an Altadis brand, or how a blender can work inside a conglomerate without handing over the marca.</p>",
  "byron": "<p><strong>Byron</strong> is one of the three brands <a href=\"/node/nelsonalfonso/\">Nelson Alfonso</a>'s <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a> brought out in 2012, along with <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a> and Bandolero. It is a revived brand: Selected Tobacco dates Byron to 1850 and celebrated its 175th anniversary in 2025.</p>\n<p>The core lines are named for centuries. Siglo XIX, Siglo XX and Siglo XXI (19th, 20th and 21st Century) are offered in a small number of sizes, and the company added the XIX Grand Poemas and XX Habaneros in 2015. For the anniversary Selected Tobacco made 175 humidors of 60 cigars, 150 of them for U.S. stores. Each held 15 apiece of the Byron 1850 No. 3, 19th Century Grand Poemas, 20th Century Habaneros and 21st Century Elegantes. Like the company's other brands, Byron is made in small batches, priced at the top of the market, and sold through a limited group of retailers.</p>\n<p>The cigars are made in Costa Rica, and this map links Byron to <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>, the factory associated with Selected Tobacco's production. Alfonso spent years as a designer for Habanos S.A., where he worked on the Cohiba Behike. That background shows in Byron's ornate bands and boxes.</p>",
  "mayflower": "<p><strong>Mayflower Cigars</strong> is the premium handmade brand launched in <strong>November 2023</strong> by <strong>Michael Knowles</strong>, host of <em>The Michael Knowles Show</em> at The Daily Wire. The name is personal history, not a marketing gimmick: four of Knowles's ancestors sailed on the Mayflower in 1620, and the brand presents itself as the culmination of a lifelong cigar habit that started with a family friend's machine-made Cuban, free sticks from rollers on Arthur Avenue in the Bronx, and a final Christmas gift of robustos from his mother. In Knowles's own telling, that same maternal gift box was made at the factory that now rolls Mayflower, <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a>'s Estelí operation.</p>\n<p>Production is at <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> in Estelí, the same floor behind <a href=\"/node/seriev/\">Oliva Serie V</a>, Melanio, <a href=\"/node/nub/\">NUB</a>, and <a href=\"/node/cain/\">Cain</a>. Knowles worked with Oliva designers on the blends; early trade coverage described the cigars as handmade in Knowles's preferred Nicaraguan factory, with logistics and DTC fulfillment through Brandshopper (parent of Cigarpage) and mayflowercigars.com. The core portfolio is built as a day-to-night arc: <strong>Dawn</strong> (mild to medium; Ecuador Connecticut wrapper, Cameroon binder, Nicaraguan long fillers), <strong>Dusk</strong> (medium to full; Ecuador Habano wrapper, Sumatra binder, Nicaraguan fillers), and <strong>Dream</strong> (double maduro; Pennsylvania Broadleaf wrapper, Mexican San Andrés binder, Nicaraguan filler, with notes the brand describes as chicory, cardamom, oak, and caramel). Limited <strong>Dawn of America</strong> (2026) reworks the Dawn recipe for the U.S. 250th anniversary with a seven-year-aged U.S. Connecticut shade wrapper over the same Cameroon/Nicaragua stack, sold in tubes of ten with a secondary band quoting the Constitution's \"to ourselves and our posterity.\"</p>\n<p>On the map, Mayflower is a clean contract-manufacturing story: a media-led brand identity, American heritage branding, and Estelí execution at industrial scale, without factory ownership. It sits on Oliva's Tabolisa node the way many outside projects do, but with an unusually public origin story (Daily Wire culture, college \"SIGAR\" club, cigar-review column, and the Pilgrim ancestry that named the marca). For enthusiasts tracing who actually rolls celebrity and media brands, Mayflower points straight to one of Nicaragua's largest vertically integrated floors rather than an anonymous Estelí address.</p>",
  "neworder": "<p><strong>New Order of the Ages (NOA)</strong> was a small factory in Estelí, Nicaragua, run by <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a> with Renegade Cigars owner Brandon Hayes from 2016 to about 2018. It made Rojas's own <a href=\"/node/guayacan/\">Guayacan</a> and <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas</a> lines, plus private-label and boutique work for other brands.</p>\n<p>NOA was Rojas's second factory. He first came to the U.S. market with Guayacan at the 2013 IPCPR show, when he owned and ran Tabacalera Aromas de Jalapa in Estelí. The partners there split in late 2014. Rojas and Hayes then opened NOA, a 5,000-square-foot factory, on January 1, 2016, and Guayacan moved with him. Guayacan Rojas Reserve had arrived in 2015, followed by a Connecticut version.</p>\n<p>NOA's outside clients included the original <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a> releases, Xiphos, Ohana, Prendelo and Kilo, a cigar made for The Cigar Authority. The arrangement didn't last. By January 2018 Jas Sum Kral had moved production to <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragón</a> in Estelí, and by mid-2018 NOA was reported closed while Rojas planned a factory in Texas.</p>\n<p>Rojas returned to Nicaragua in 2021 with a third factory, a converted Estelí movie theater now called the <a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas Cigar Factory</a>. An earlier version of this map said NOA cigars were rolled at A.J. Fernández. We found no source for that, so the link has been removed.</p>",
  "acid": "<p><strong>Acid</strong> is the infused cigar line made by <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> at <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua. It is the brand that made the company famous, and since 2014 it has belonged to <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher International</a>, Drew Estate's owner.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/node/jonathandrew/\">Jonathan Drew</a> and Marvin Samel started Drew Estate in New York in 1996, and their first brand was La Vieja Habana, not Acid. In 1998 Drew moved to Estelí and rebuilt production there after Hurricane Mitch.</p>\n<p>Acid followed in 1999, created with the artist Scott \"Acid\" Chester. The name comes from his design studio, Arielle Chester Industrial Design (ACID), and his urban art style shaped the boxes and the color-coded labels. Drew Estate calls the cigars infused rather than flavored. The tobacco is aged with botanicals and oils in aroma rooms, and the process is kept secret. Familiar Acid cigars include Kuba Kuba, a Sumatra-wrapped blend, and Blondie, a small Connecticut-wrapped cigar with a pigtail cap, along with Cold Infusion and 1400cc.</p>\n<p>La Gran Fábrica opened in July 2007 and, according to Cigar Journal, makes about 100,000 cigars a day. Swisher agreed to buy Drew Estate in October 2014. The same factory makes Drew Estate's traditional, uninfused lines, <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a> and <a href=\"/node/undercrown/\">Undercrown</a>. Infused cigars and some of Nicaragua's most sought-after traditional ones come from the same floor.</p>",
  "diesel": "<p><strong>Diesel</strong> is a full-bodied brand made by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> in Nicaragua. It debuted in 2009 through Cigars International and Cigar.com as a catalog and mail-order brand. The first and, for a while, only size was the Unholy Cocktail, a 5 x 56 belicoso. Shorty and the Unlimited line followed, and Diesel moved into brick-and-mortar shops in 2017.</p>\n<p>Later releases include Grind (2017) and Whiskey Row (2018), a cigar aged in bourbon barrels. The seasonal Sunday Gravy series began in 2021 with San Marzano and was followed by Pecorino, Rosamarino and Grappa, with a sixth and final blend scheduled for October 2022. The newer releases are handmade at Tabacalera A.J. Fernández in Estelí and were blended by Fernández with Justin Andrews. <a href=\"/node/manowar/\">Man O' War</a> is a sibling A.J. Fernández brand.</p>\n<p>Diesel is owned within <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, which also owns Cigars International. When STG created <a href=\"/node/forged/\">The Forged Cigar Company</a> in 2021, Diesel was one of its first five brands, and it was still on Forged's list in 2026. Cigar Aficionado reported in 2016 that Diesel was planned for the new <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal; the newer Sunday Gravy releases are made at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> in Estelí.</p>",
  "undercrown": "<p><strong>Undercrown</strong> is made by <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> at <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua. It is the company's traditional maduro brand, and it started as the cigar the rollers smoked.</p>\n<p>Around 2009, demand for <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a> was outrunning supply, and the torcedores were asked to stop smoking Ligas on the factory floor. They built their own blend from other primings of the same rare tobaccos. Drew Estate released that factory smoke in 2011. The original, now called Undercrown Maduro, uses a Mexican San Andrés wrapper, a Connecticut River Valley stalk-cut Habano binder, and Brazilian Mata Fina and Nicaraguan filler.</p>\n<p>The line grew in stages. Shade followed in 2015, blended by Willy Herrera: an Ecuador Connecticut wrapper over a Sumatra binder. Sun Grown arrived in 2017 with an Ecuador sun-grown Sumatra wrapper, and Undercrown 10 marked the brand's tenth year.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/node/jonathandrew/\">Jonathan Drew</a>'s company put the rollers' blend on the market. <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher</a> has owned Drew Estate since 2014, and La Gran Fábrica, opened in July 2007, makes about 100,000 cigars a day, according to Cigar Journal. Liga Privada is the scarce broadleaf line. Undercrown is the one the factory floor made for itself, and it sits alongside <a href=\"/node/acid/\">Acid</a> and <a href=\"/node/deadwood/\">Deadwood</a> in the same factory.</p>",
  "manowar": "<p><strong>Man O' War</strong> is made by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> at his <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal, Nicaragua. It is a full-bodied brand he blended for the catalog retailers Cigars International and Cigar.com, which are part of <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>.</p>\n<p>It came out in 2009, the same year as its sibling <a href=\"/node/diesel/\">Diesel</a>. For years the line was sold only through those two retailers, which made it one of the best-known catalog-exclusive brands.</p>\n<p>Ruination, reviewed by Cigars International in May 2009, uses Cuban-seed Nicaraguan and Honduran ligero under an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper and is built for strength. Virtue arrived in 2010 as the milder line, swapping much of the ligero for seco and viso leaves under a Connecticut-style wrapper. The core Man O' War sits between the two.</p>\n<p>In 2017 Cigar Aficionado reported that San Lotano, which Fernández had bought in October 2016 and opened that January, was producing \"the entire high-volume portfolios\" of Man O' War and Ave Maria. It described both as catalog brands owned by Cigars International. San Lotano also makes 601 and Murciélago for Espinosa. When STG created <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged</a> in 2021, Diesel moved to that sales arm; Man O' War was not among Forged's first brands.</p>",
  "seriev": "<p><strong>Serie V</strong> is made by <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a> at <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a>, its factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. It is a full-bodied Nicaraguan blend built around ligero filler under a sun-grown Habano wrapper, and it is the cigar that made Oliva a critics' brand.</p>\n<p>Cigar Aficionado covered its launch in 2007. Before it, Oliva was best known for value cigars. In August 2012 Oliva added Serie V Melanio, named for Melanio Oliva, the family's tobacco-growing patriarch in Cuba. Melanio uses an Ecuadorian Sumatra-seed wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler, and it smokes a step smoother than the original. The Melanio Figurado was Cigar Aficionado's Cigar of the Year in 2014, with 96 points. Melanio Maduro is the darker version.</p>\n<p>In 2016 the Belgian company J. Cortès, owned by the Vandermarliere family, bought Oliva, and José Oliva stayed on as CEO. The blend and the factory stayed in Estelí. J. Cortès later rebuilt Tabolisa, which it says can make about 90,000 cigars a day.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/node/nub/\">NUB</a> and <a href=\"/node/cain/\">Cain</a> are separate Oliva brands from the same factory, both created with Sam Leccia and later put under Oliva's Studio Tobac umbrella. Serie V remains the flagship, a family company's best-known cigar that outlasted the family's ownership.</p>",
  "brickhouse": "<p><strong>Brick House</strong> is a <a href=\"/node/jcnewman/\">J.C. Newman</a> brand named for the house in Hungary where founder Julius Caesar Newman was born. It was the only brick house in his small village, and family, friends and neighbors gathered there every night. Newman introduced Brick House cigars in 1937. His grandsons, Eric and Bobby Newman, reintroduced the brand in 2009 as a Nicaraguan cigar.</p>\n<p>The relaunched Brick House was first made at the San Rafael factory owned by Fidel Olivas, which made El Baton and Brick House for J.C. Newman until the company moved them to its own factory. A.J. Fernández later bought that building and reopened it in 2017 as the <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano factory</a>. Brick House is now rolled at J.C. Newman PENSA (Puros de Estelí Nicaragua S.A.), which J.C. Newman calls the second-largest cigar factory in Nicaragua. It has more than 800 employees and also makes Quorum and Perla del Mar.</p>\n<p>Brick House Classic uses an Ecuadorian Havana wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler. Maduro uses a Brazilian Arapiraca wrapper. Double Connecticut pairs a Connecticut Shade wrapper with a Connecticut Broadleaf binder, and Bricktoberfest is a limited release. J.C. Newman's ultra-premium <a href=\"/node/diamondcrown/\">Diamond Crown</a> is a different story: it is made in the Dominican Republic at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a>.</p>",
  "bolivar_nc": "<p><strong>Bolivar (non-Cuban)</strong> is made in Danlí, Honduras, at <a href=\"/node/stgdanli/\">STG Danlí (HATSA)</a>, and sold in the U.S. by <a href=\"/node/forged/\">Forged Cigar Co.</a>, part of <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. It is not the Cuban Bolivar of Habanos S.A. <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> has held the U.S. trademark for decades, in the same Cifuentes-name family as <a href=\"/node/partagas_nc/\">Partagás (non-Cuban)</a>.</p>\n<p>The line people still buy is Cofradia. Trade reports on recent releases put it at HATSA: the Cofradia Oscuro (May 2024) and the 6 x 60 Cofradia Gigante (February 2025) were both made there. Cofradia uses an Ecuadorian Sumatra-seed wrapper, a Connecticut broadleaf binder, and Honduran and Nicaraguan filler, and now comes in five sizes. Cofradia Oscuro flips the wrapper and binder (Connecticut broadleaf over Ecuadorian Sumatra) and adds Dominican filler. In 2022 Robert Caldwell and STG's Justin Andrews made two limited Cofradia Lost &amp; Found blends at HATSA from 5- to 8-year-old bales in the factory's tobacco library.</p>\n<p>General relaunched the marca in 2005 and credited Daniel Núñez, Benji Menendez, Estelo Padrón (brother of <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a> and longtime HATSA manager) and <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a>, who was still at General then. Forged has sold Bolivar since February 2021, and the brand stayed with Forged in the February 2026 portfolio shuffle. In late 2024 STG dropped the regular Bolivar line and kept Cofradia and Gran Republica. HATSA also makes non-Cuban <a href=\"/node/hoyonc/\">Hoyo de Monterrey</a>.</p>",
};
